-- must match; identity, type and attribution drift abort all changes.
-- Empty installations skip absent courses. Media and old attempt answer keys stay intact.
DO $quality$
DECLARE
  v_plan jsonb := '[{"course_id":"64c67e86-4327-42e1-bef3-7841c70719d6","course_title":"AI Bootcamp for Autonomous Driving","additions":[{"target_capsule_id":"1f54f8da-ce1c-43f5-9edc-da3effd66ad2","title":"Study guide: evaluate a simulated AEB learning system","content_type":"text","content_value":"# Study guide: evaluate a simulated AEB learning system\n\n## Goal and prerequisites\n\nUse the existing ML-based AEB project as a small engineering investigation: can a learned brake decision improve on a declared baseline on held-out simulator episodes? The deliverable is a reproducible evaluation, not a controller ready for a vehicle. Work entirely with offline data and simulation.\n\nYou need Python functions, lists, CSV handling, basic probability and a working understanding of classification. Revisit **Python Basics**, **Data Analysis Basics**, **How Machines Learn** and **Logistic Regression** when those concepts are unfamiliar. A CPU is enough for the calculation below. Existing simulator and neural-network notebooks may have separate dependencies or accelerator requirements; record the actual versions used rather than assuming every environment matches.\n\n## Follow a sequence with evidence at each step\n\n1. In **Foundations of Machine Learning**, write down the prediction target, the features available at decision time and the source of labels. Save a short data dictionary with units.\n2. In **Supervised Machine Learning**, run the AEB project and retain a simple reference rule alongside the learned classifier. Keep speed in metres per second and distance in metres; a silent km/h conversion error changes the physical problem.\n3. In **Motion Prediction and Planning**, identify who consumes the model output. A classifier score is not a trajectory or a brake command. Specify the threshold, command limits, missing-data behavior and execution period separately.\n4. Use **Deep Learning and Reinforcement Learning** for a comparison only after fixing the evaluation protocol. A learned policy still depends on designed observations, rewards and environment rules. Reuse the same held-out scenarios when comparing approaches.\n5. Treat the later vision and generative projects as extensions. An attractive detection result or generated scene does not establish that the braking pipeline handles a hazard correctly.\n\n## Define the experiment before training\n\nCreate an episode table containing `episode_id`, simulator seed, speed, gap, closing speed, label source and split. Nearby frames from the same drive can be strongly related; keep complete episodes in one split. Reserve separate training, validation and final test episodes. Use validation data to select the threshold and freeze it before the final test. MetaDrive explicitly supports distinct scenario sets for training and evaluation in its [environment documentation](https://metadrive-simulator.readthedocs.io/en/latest/rl_environments.html).\n\nFit learned preprocessing on the training partition only. Keep preprocessing and prediction together when using a scikit-learn pipeline; see its [data-leakage guidance](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html). Do not use future collision outcomes as an input feature available to a present-time decision.\n\nIf labels come from a teacher rule, report that fact. Matching a teacher measures imitation of that rule. It does not independently validate the teacher or prove collision avoidance.\n\n## Runnable exercise: why accuracy can mislead\n\nRun this with Python 3; no packages or downloads are needed. Here `1` means the reference label requests braking. These are invented evaluation counts, not measurements from the supplied project.\n\n```python\ntruth = [1] * 10 + [0] * 990\nnever_brake = [0] * 1000\ncandidate = [1] * 8 + [0] * 2 + [1] * 20 + [0] * 970\n\ndef evaluate(labels, predictions):\n    assert len(labels) == len(predictions)\n    tp = sum(y == 1 and p == 1 for y, p in zip(labels, predictions))\n    fn = sum(y == 1 and p == 0 for y, p in zip(labels, predictions))\n    fp = sum(y == 0 and p == 1 for y, p in zip(labels, predictions))\n    tn = sum(y == 0 and p == 0 for y, p in zip(labels, predictions))\n    return {\n        \"accuracy\": (tp + tn) / len(labels),\n        \"recall\": tp / (tp + fn) if tp + fn else None,\n        \"precision\": tp / (tp + fp) if tp + fp else None,\n        \"missed_braking_events\": fn,\n    }\n\nbaseline = evaluate(truth, never_brake)\nresult = evaluate(truth, candidate)\nprint(\"baseline\", baseline)\nprint(\"candidate\", result)\nassert baseline[\"accuracy\"] == 0.99 and baseline[\"recall\"] == 0.0\nassert result[\"missed_braking_events\"] == 2\n```\n\nThe baseline is 99% accurate while missing every positive example. The candidate detects 8 of 10 positives, but only 8 of its 28 brake predictions match positive labels: recall is 0.8 and precision is about 0.286. Neither result alone establishes an acceptable controller. Read the [official metric definitions](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.precision_recall_fscore_support.html), then explain which errors matter for your experiment and why.\n\n## Capstone submission and failure analysis\n\nSubmit the data dictionary, episode split list, environment/version record, baseline, selected model and threshold, runnable evaluation command and a failure table. Inspect at least one false negative, one false positive and one case with missing or delayed inputs. State whether evaluation counts represent frames or independent hazard events; counting many adjacent frames can exaggerate the amount of evidence. Report event counts alongside rates, including how few hazardous episodes were tested.\n\nUse this 100-point rubric: problem/units/label specification (20), split and preprocessing discipline (25), baseline and error analysis (25), reproducibility (20), and an honest statement of limits (10). A result with a lower headline accuracy can earn full marks when its experiment is valid and its limitations are clear. Keep the final test separate even when its result is disappointing.","id":"a35d9d83-4e49-5ca4-a4b9-2dfc5237b70b"}],"capsule_updates":[{"id":"dde43f20-164c-47be-a89e-3e131104d6b1","before_description":"Searching, sorting and pathfinding. The pathfinding here returns later as global planning — this is the same A* wearing different clothes.","description":"A notebook for practising machine-learning algorithms. Run the examples, identify each learning task and compare assumptions and evaluation metrics with the lecture material.","learning_path_id":"497c1e08-9802-4c86-a491-881e5a5957bd"},{"id":"b4e0a008-5ee5-43c8-92d8-db45860a021c","before_description":"Why an ensemble beats any single model, and when it does not. This is the most reliable free win in applied ML.","description":"Explore how combining estimators can improve predictive performance, and compare the added complexity and evaluation results with a single-model baseline.","learning_path_id":"c9af247f-52c4-405a-a515-ec585217b2dc"},{"id":"3ecebff9-89fb-4799-a437-69b87d4b66a5","before_description":"The classifier every other classifier is measured against. If your neural network cannot beat it, the network is not the problem.","description":"Use logistic regression as a classification baseline, then compare its assumptions and held-out performance with more complex models.","learning_path_id":"c9af247f-52c4-405a-a515-ec585217b2dc"},{"id":"4523f54c-06f8-48d5-a2b0-e9a67966dffc","before_description":"An actor-critic agent that learns to drive end to end. No planner, no rules — just reward, and it is unsettling how well it works.","description":"Train a PPO actor-critic policy in MetaDrive. Examine how the reward, observations, action space and simulator rules shape behavior, then evaluate held-out scenarios.","learning_path_id":"6114a8dd-8dd1-4bf2-8085-d63ded25bdc8"},{"id":"56dd7a30-3797-4d6a-abf9-8114dc9fc1ed","before_description":"A CNN on a real benchmark, with real class imbalance and real degraded images. The gap between a curated dataset and GTSRB is the gap between a paper and a product.","description":"Train and evaluate a traffic-sign classifier using GTSRB. Inspect per-class errors and keep evaluation data separate from model and threshold selection.","learning_path_id":"6114a8dd-8dd1-4bf2-8085-d63ded25bdc8"},{"id":"6952ae8a-ebaa-45b0-97ab-4a1b1b8e5aed","before_description":"Automatic Emergency Braking, trained and driven in the MetaDrive simulator. The first time your model failing means the car hits something.","description":"Study a machine-learning-based emergency-braking project in MetaDrive, with attention to missed hazards, unnecessary interventions and the limits of simulator evidence.","learning_path_id":"c9af247f-52c4-405a-a515-ec585217b2dc"}],"content_updates":[{"id":"7f42d378-1796-4d9b-bd58-e4b9bc2650e0","before_content_value":"# Welcome\n\nYou are here to build AI systems for autonomous driving, not to collect algorithms. That distinction shapes everything about how this course is arranged.\n\n## What you will actually do\n\nBy the end you will have trained an emergency braking system that decides when to stop a car, a CNN that reads traffic signs, an RL agent that drives with no hand-written rules at all, a perception stack running on real KITTI data, and six projects on the current frontier — fine-tuned LLMs, an agentic system, a vision-language model reasoning over a driving scene, and controllable image generation.\n\n## How it is arranged\n\nSeven modules, and the order is the argument:\n\n1. **Foundations** — Python, data structures, algorithms, SQL\n2. **Foundations of ML** — how a machine learns anything at all\n3. **Supervised ML** — the classical models, and your first real feature (AEB)\n4. **Motion prediction and planning** — connecting perception to action\n5. **Deep learning and RL** — when hand-written features run out\n6. **Computer vision** — earning the assumption that the car can see\n7. **Multimodal LLMs and generative vision** — where the field is going\n\nEach module opens with a \"What this module covers\" lesson explaining why it follows the one before it. Read those. They are the thread.\n\n## Who this is for\n\nEngineers who can already program and want to build systems. If you are looking for a gentle introduction to AI, this is not it — Module 3 assumes you can wrangle data without being told how.\n\n**Start with Module 1.** If you are already fluent in Python, skim it and move on — but do not skip the algorithms notebook. The pathfinding in it comes back as global planning.","content_value":"# Welcome\n\nThis course connects programming, machine learning and simulation-based autonomous-driving projects. Its seven technical modules cover programming; ML foundations; supervised learning and a simulated AEB project; prediction and planning; deep learning and reinforcement learning; computer vision; and multimodal/generative AI.\n\nThe catalogue includes lectures, notebooks, project repositories and reference material. Work through the module introductions, run the relevant artifacts and record what you can reproduce in your environment. The accompanying study guide provides a shared evaluation task and submission rubric.\n\nThe projects are learning exercises. A successful notebook, classifier or simulator policy does not establish readiness for road use. Learned policies also depend on designed rewards, observations and environment rules; they are not rule-free systems.\n\nStart with programming foundations if you need Python, data structures or data-analysis practice. Otherwise, check those skills and continue to the ML foundations. Keep a record of dependencies, data splits, baselines and failures as you progress.","capsule_id":"1f54f8da-ce1c-43f5-9edc-da3effd66ad2","before_title":"Welcome"},{"id":"ca30d049-f6a3-4e05-9cca-1602ed70b232","before_content_value":"## Start here\n\nClassical ML got you a working AEB. Deep learning is what happens when the features are too complicated to write down by hand — which, for driving, they always are.\n\n- **Deep Learning and Reinforcement Learning — Lecture** — neural networks, then policies that learn by acting\n- **PyTorch: From Foundations to Deployment** — the framework, taken all the way to a deployable model\n- **Traffic Sign Classification (GTSRB)** — a real CNN on a real benchmark\n- **RL Actor-Critic PPO in MetaDrive** — a PPO agent that drives end to end, no hand-written planner anywhere\n\n**Why it matters:** the PPO notebook is the first time you build something with *no explicit rules at all* — the policy is learned from reward. Sitting it directly beside the rule-based pipeline from Module 3 is the point: you should come away able to argue for either.\n\n**Next:** Module 5 goes back to the sensor and asks what the network actually sees.","content_value":"## Deep learning and reinforcement learning\n\nThe lecture introduces neural networks and policies learned through interaction. The linked PyTorch and GTSRB notebooks provide practice with models and image classification; the MetaDrive PPO project introduces an actor-critic driving policy.\n\nDeep learning can learn useful representations, but it does not remove the need for data checks, suitable targets or careful evaluation. Reinforcement learning depends on the specified observations, actions, rewards and environment dynamics. Review these definitions in the [RL concepts reference](https://spinningup.openai.com/en/latest/spinningup/rl_intro.html).\n\nCompare a learned policy with a declared baseline on the same held-out simulator scenarios. Measure failures and execution requirements as well as reward. Neither learned nor hand-designed control is automatically safer or better for every task.\n\nNext, examine the computer-vision resources and the assumptions introduced when inputs come from images.","capsule_id":"f8cc1c01-9245-40dd-83c5-03f51d375377","before_title":"What this module covers"},{"id":"b1bac4a5-b42a-4e0d-be33-59534b734929","before_content_value":"# What you have built\n\nLook back at what is now in your repository.\n\n- An **AEB system** that brakes a car in simulation, trained from data\n- A **CNN** that classifies traffic signs on a real benchmark\n- A **PPO agent** that drives end to end, learned entirely from reward\n- A **perception stack** — detection, tracking, segmentation, lane estimation, speed from vision — on real KITTI data\n- **Six frontier projects**: a pretrained and fine-tuned LLM, efficient fine-tuning, an agentic system, a VLM reasoning over a driving scene, and controllable generation\n\nThat is not a portfolio of tutorials. It is a portfolio of systems.\n\n## What to do next\n\n**Put the projects somewhere public.** The PPO agent and the KITTI work are the two that make a hiring manager stop scrolling. A repository with a clear README beats a certificate every time.\n\n**Go deeper where it caught you.** If the AEB and PPO work is what you enjoyed, you are a controls person and did not know it. If KITTI and segmentation held your attention, you are a perception person.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\n**Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS): Engineering & Development** is the natural next step. This course taught you the AI; ADAS teaches you how it becomes a product — functional safety, ISO 26262, requirements, the V-model, and a Simulink capstone co-simulated against IPG CarMaker. It is the difference between a model that works and a feature that ships, and it is the gap most ML engineers never cross.\n\n**Modern Vehicle Control (PID → LQR → MPC)** is the other direction — if the RL agent made you want to know what a *provably* good controller looks like, that is the course that answers it.\n\nCongratulations. Go and build something.","content_value":"# Review your evidence\n\nThe course provides projects involving simulated AEB, traffic-sign classification, reinforcement learning, perception and multimodal or generative AI. Before describing a project as complete, check that you can reproduce its run, explain its inputs and outputs, and show evaluation on data or scenarios held out from development.\n\nFor each completed project, keep a README with versions, data permissions, a baseline, metrics and a failure analysis. Clearly distinguish implemented results from planned extensions. Publish only material you have permission to share.\n\nThe ADAS course extends the learning sequence into requirements, architecture, controls and a simulated capstone workflow. The Vehicle Dynamics and Control course provides additional study of PID, LQR and MPC. These methods have different assumptions and validation needs; no method is universally best or automatically safe.\n\nUse the study-guide rubric to choose your next improvement: clearer experiment design, stronger tests, better reproducibility or a more precise account of the limits.","capsule_id":"9d0928d9-5b55-4a3e-b1f6-97c4a7ce426c","before_title":"What next"}],"question_updates":[{"id":"00941394-9e1e-40cc-82e2-e34d63a744fb","before_question_text":"Detection finds objects in a frame. Tracking additionally:","before_options":{"a":"Improves accuracy","b":"Associates the same object across frames, giving it an identity and a velocity","c":"Segments pixels","d":"Estimates depth"},"before_correct_answer":"b","before_explanation":"Without identity there is no velocity, and without velocity a planner cannot predict anything. Detection alone is not enough to drive.","question_text":"Compared with per-frame object detection, multi-object tracking typically adds:","options":{"a":"A guarantee that every object is detected.","b":"Associations across frames and temporal state estimates for tracked objects.","c":"Pixel-perfect segmentation for every object.","d":"A guarantee of accurate depth for every object."},"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"Tracking links observations over time and can estimate motion for persistent tracks. Object identity is not a prerequisite for every velocity measurement: Doppler radar can estimate radial velocity. See https://www.mathworks.com/help/radar/ug/doppler-estimation.html.","quiz_id":"a379cea8-2d19-463e-9993-5720a3cd269f","question_type":"mcq"},{"id":"03fb8f77-7cc3-4893-b0b3-e1dcddbf8c1e","before_question_text":"Why do most production ADAS features use classical ML rather than deep networks?","before_options":{"a":"They are more accurate","b":"They are cheaper to run, easier to certify, and often enough","c":"Deep learning does not work on cars","d":"They need no data"},"before_correct_answer":"b","before_explanation":"Latency budgets are hard and the safety case must be arguable. \"It is simpler and it passes\" beats \"it scored higher on the benchmark\".","question_text":"Which result could justify choosing a classical model over a deep network for a particular ADAS subtask?","options":{"a":"Classical models are always more accurate.","b":"The model meets measured task and timing requirements with a simpler validated implementation.","c":"Deep networks cannot process driving data.","d":"The model requires no training data or evaluation."},"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"Select a model using evidence for the specific task and implementation constraints. Neither classical nor deep models are universally superior, and model choice alone does not certify a feature. Compare measured latency and predictive performance; see https://scikit-learn.org/stable/computing/computational_performance.html.","quiz_id":"4ff00b53-d95b-4042-83d4-a008acdf18fd","question_type":"mcq"},{"id":"05b4dd39-bbdd-4b4b-8808-83456da18b99","before_question_text":"Most machine learning failures in production are caused by a bug in the model code.","before_options":null,"before_correct_answer":"false","before_explanation":"Most are failures of the assumptions underneath — leaked labels, a train/serve skew, a distribution that shifted, a metric that did not measure what anyone cared about. The code is usually fine.","question_text":"Bug-free model code guarantees reliable predictions in a new driving environment.","options":null,"correct_answer":"false","explanation":"A system can fail because the evaluation protocol leaked information, preprocessing differs at inference, or the new data differs from the development distribution. Correct code alone does not establish generalization. See https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html.","quiz_id":"e82ea585-3c07-454a-a13b-dcf0bbf94565","question_type":"true_false"}],"quizzes":[{"capsule_id":"0730b9ad-1532-4fd0-ad19-f8fc055c7df1","title":"Engineering check: AEB experiment design","questions":[{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A simulated drive produces many adjacent frames. Which split best tests performance on new drives?","options":{"a":"Assign whole drive episodes to separate training, validation and test sets.","b":"Shuffle all frames and split individual frames at random.","c":"Put each episode in all three sets to balance conditions.","d":"Fit preprocessing on every frame, then split episodes."},"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Keeping related frames within one partition reduces leakage across development and evaluation. Fit learned preprocessing using training data only.","points":1,"id":"fdceb1ff-b012-5d52-abad-cabcef72a042","order_index":0},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A brake classifier detects 8 reference hazards, misses 2, and produces 20 false alarms. What is its positive-class precision?","options":{"a":"8/10, or 80%","b":"20/28, or about 71%","c":"2/10, or 20%","d":"8/28, or about 28.6%"},"correct_answer":"d","explanation":"Precision is true positives divided by all positive predictions: 8/(8+20). Recall is 8/(8+2), a different quantity.","points":1,"id":"d567c769-2812-5e2c-ae1f-7da6c8f95ea7","order_index":1},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A classifier reaches 99% accuracy on data containing 1% hazard labels. What should be checked before calling this useful?","options":{"a":"Whether accuracy is written to enough decimal places.","b":"Whether the classifier was trained with a deep network.","c":"Hazard recall, false alarms and the behavior of a never-brake baseline.","d":"Whether the test labels can be added to its input features."},"correct_answer":"c","explanation":"A never-brake classifier is already 99% accurate on this class distribution and has zero hazard recall. Inspect the confusion matrix and event-level failures.","points":1,"id":"6706444e-9599-538a-a6c0-5db2bb25f991","order_index":2},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A learned AEB model closely matches labels generated by a hand-written stopping rule. What conclusion does this directly support?","options":{"a":"The model is independently validated as safe for road use.","b":"The model imitates that teacher on the evaluated data.","c":"The teacher is correct in all unseen driving conditions.","d":"Sensor delays and action limits can be ignored."},"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"Agreement measures imitation of the chosen label source. Independent scenario tests and examination of the teacher assumptions are still needed.","points":1,"id":"28d6b52b-ddde-5cb5-ae14-44af799f916c","order_index":3}],"id":"10ebd9a0-66bd-542a-ac78-c6ff0fac37fb"}]},{"course_id":"9078b1a0-fc12-4808-ad21-98ed4e94b70c","course_title":"Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS): Engineering & Development","additions":[{"target_capsule_id":"5a1f1b4f-32ff-4f2c-b13a-0064b10474f4","title":"Capstone brief: requirements and evidence for simulated AEB, ACC and LKA","content_type":"text","content_value":"# Capstone brief: requirements and evidence for simulated AEB, ACC and LKA\n\n## Scope and prerequisites\n\nBuild an inspectable simulation exercise linking the course''s AEB, ACC and lane-keeping material to requirements and tests. The result is a learning artifact with explicit assumptions, not a certified road-going feature. AEB, ACC and LKA do not by themselves establish autonomous-driving capability: [NHTSA distinguishes driver assistance from higher automation](https://www.nhtsa.gov/vehicle-safety/automated-vehicle-safety).\n\nBefore starting, review the programming primers, supervised-learning/AEB project, motion-planning modules and longitudinal/lateral control lectures. You should be able to read a time series, work with metres and seconds and explain a feedback loop. The existing final project uses MATLAB/Simulink and IPG CarMaker; confirm that you have the required software, licenses and compatible versions. The calculation below requires only Python 3 and remains useful when those tools are unavailable.\n\n## Work from a requirement to an observable result\n\nStart with the **ADAS Requirement Writing Assignment**, attempt it before opening its solution, then compare the assumptions and testability of your statements. For the capstone, give every requirement an identifier, enabling conditions, expected output, timing requirement and test reference. Replace vague wording such as “brake safely” with a bounded statement whose outcome can be measured in the declared model.\n\nA toy example is: “R-AEB-01: in the stationary-target model below, a braking request issued at a 20 m gap with ego speed 10 m/s shall leave at least 2 m clearance, assuming 0.3 s response delay followed by constant 5 m/s² deceleration.” The test must report the actual delay, stopping position and minimum clearance. These are invented classroom parameters, not a recommended AEB calibration.\n\nCreate an interface table for each block: signal name, unit, coordinate frame, timestamp, valid range and invalid-data indication. A range without its age can be misleading. Keep feature activation, controller output and actuator command separate so that an apparently correct controller cannot hide a downstream interface fault.\n\n## Calculate an envelope before tuning\n\nFor a stationary target, level ground, initial speed `v`, response delay `tau` and constant positive braking magnitude `a`, the simplified stopping distance is `v*tau + v*v/(2*a)`. It follows by adding constant-speed travel during delay to the distance obtained by integrating constant deceleration. This model omits tire saturation, grade, changing targets and actuator buildup after the assumed delay.\n\nRun this sweep and inspect which conditions exceed your available gap:\n\n```python\nfrom itertools import product\n\ndef required_gap(v, delay, decel, margin=2.0):\n    assert v >= 0 and delay >= 0 and decel > 0\n    return v * delay + v * v / (2.0 * decel) + margin\n\nassert abs(required_gap(10.0, 0.3, 5.0) - 15.0) < 1e-9\nfor v, delay, decel in product((5.0, 10.0, 15.0), (0.1, 0.3), (3.0, 5.0)):\n    gap = required_gap(v, delay, decel)\n    print(f\"v={v:4.1f} delay={delay:.1f} decel={decel:.1f} required_gap={gap:6.2f} m\")\n```\n\nAt 10 m/s, the stated delay and deceleration require 13 m to stop, or 15 m including the example margin. A physically infeasible test case should be identified as infeasible under these assumptions; changing a controller gain cannot create unavailable braking distance. Extend the sweep to the boundaries just inside and outside each requirement''s operating conditions.\n\n## Integrate features and test failures deliberately\n\nImplement an explicit command-arbitration policy. For this exercise, define how an AEB braking request overrides an ACC acceleration request, how outputs are bounded and how a feature is inhibited after an invalid input. Document the policy rather than assuming that combining independently working blocks will produce it automatically.\n\nUse the existing planning projects to distinguish route, behavior and local trajectory decisions. Use the control projects to track an admissible reference. Add cases for a stationary lead vehicle, lead-vehicle deceleration, a cut-in, stale range data, missing lane information and command saturation. Parameterized scenario descriptions can make experiments reproducible; [ASAM OpenSCENARIO XML](https://www.asam.net/standards/detail/openscenario-xml/) is a relevant industry standard to explore, but using it is optional here.\n\n## Build an evidence pack, not just a demonstration video\n\nRun model-level tests first. Where the toolchain is available, replay the same inputs against generated code in SIL and compare outputs using stated tolerances. [MathWorks defines SIL as execution of generated code on the development computer](https://www.mathworks.com/help/ecoder/software-in-the-loop-sil-simulation.html). Agreement checks numerical consistency between those implementations; shared model mistakes can still pass. Target-processor execution and hardware effects require additional evidence beyond host SIL, as explained in its [SIL/PIL overview](https://www.mathworks.com/help/ecoder/ug/about-sil-and-pil-simulations.html).\n\nSubmit requirements, interface definitions, architecture/arbitration diagram, test parameters and seeds, recorded traces, pass/fail calculations and a defect log. For each failure, distinguish an incorrect requirement, a poor plant assumption, a software defect and an integration problem. Include one unresolved limitation and its proposed next experiment.\n\nScore the work out of 100: measurable requirements and traceability (25), interfaces and arbitration (20), boundary/fault tests (25), reproducible results or justified tool limitations (20), and thoughtful failure analysis (10). A passing classroom rubric is not a functional-safety assessment or permission to test on public roads.","id":"3fe773e1-d673-5917-ad75-6fb80c52d0aa"}],"capsule_updates":[{"id":"03e180d1-7eed-443b-b871-3e5b3caa7594","before_description":"A PPO agent that learns to drive from reward alone. Nothing here is hand-written — no rules, no planner — so hold it against the rule-based stack you build next and be ready to argue for either.","description":"Explore a PPO driving policy in MetaDrive. Compare its reward, observations and held-out scenario performance with a declared baseline; simulator success does not establish road readiness.","learning_path_id":"f7560d02-ba0a-4c22-9838-63e97b081dbb"},{"id":"41c43401-5bd9-443e-ad19-b764a7223ed0","before_description":"Build the test harness, run model- and software-in-the-loop tests, then generate embedded code. This is the step that separates a model that works from a feature that ships.","description":"Study model-level testing, generated-code execution in SIL and code generation. Compare outputs under stated tolerances and distinguish host-based tests from evidence on target hardware.","learning_path_id":"18e49daf-6eee-44ef-b96e-f27004addb65"}],"content_updates":[{"id":"cb70d306-eaaf-4a8f-a5e8-3fc292b4efea","before_content_value":"# Welcome\n\nThis course is about how ADAS systems are actually built — not how they are described in a paper.\n\n## What you will actually do\n\nEleven projects and a capstone. You will build Automatic Emergency Braking from data, classify traffic signs with a CNN, train an RL agent to drive, assemble a full three-layer planning stack (A* → FSM → lattice planner), write an Adaptive Cruise Controller in C++, and finish by building AEB, ACC and Lane Keep Assist in Simulink, co-simulated against IPG CarMaker and validated with MIL/SIL testing and generated embedded code.\n\n## How it is arranged\n\nThirteen modules, following the shape of the real thing:\n\n**Foundations** → the lab and toolchain, then the programming\n**Perception** → machine learning, deep learning, reinforcement learning\n**Planning** → route, behaviour, trajectory\n**Implementation** → modern C++, the language it ships in\n**Control** → longitudinal, then lateral vehicle dynamics\n**Safety** → ISO 26262, the V-model, and why any of this is allowed on a road\n**Capstone** → all of it, run as an industry workflow\n\nEach module opens with a \"What this module covers\" lesson explaining why it follows the last. That thread is the course.\n\n## Who this is for\n\nEngineers who want to work on ADAS as it is practised, with the process and the safety case, not just the algorithms. Set up MATLAB and IPG CarMaker in Module 1 — the capstone depends on it, and you do not want to discover that in week eleven.\n\n**Start with Module 1.**","content_value":"# Welcome\n\nThis course introduces an ADAS development workflow through programming, machine learning, prediction/planning, C++, vehicle dynamics, functional-safety concepts and a simulation-based capstone.\n\nThe listed projects include ML-based AEB, image classification, PPO in MetaDrive, planning, ACC in C++ and a final AEB/ACC/LKA workflow using Simulink and IPG CarMaker. Treat these as opportunities to build and evaluate engineering examples; completing them does not establish a production safety case or deployment approval.\n\nStart with the SDV lab material and confirm access to the software required for your chosen projects. The final co-simulation and code-generation exercises depend on the relevant licensed products and compatible versions. Keep your tool versions and assumptions with your work.\n\nUse the capstone brief on this page to connect requirements, interfaces, controller behavior and test evidence. Review each module introduction before the lecture and project resources. If you already know a foundation topic, demonstrate the prerequisite skill before moving ahead.","capsule_id":"5a1f1b4f-32ff-4f2c-b13a-0064b10474f4","before_title":"Welcome"},{"id":"6a1e6768-7065-4463-9e19-f794670e0d5c","before_content_value":"## Start here\n\nThe part that separates a demo from a product.\n\n- **Functional safety — a high-level overview** — hazard analysis, ASIL, the V-model\n- **Functional safety — implementation steps** — what it actually demands of your code and your process\n\n**Why it matters:** ISO 26262 is why ADAS engineering looks nothing like ML engineering. You cannot ship a feature because it performed well on a test set; you have to argue it is safe, and be able to show the argument. Nearly every ADAS interview goes here.\n\n**Next:** the capstone — all of it, at once.","content_value":"## Systems engineering and functional-safety concepts\n\nThese introductory resources cover hazard analysis, ASIL concepts, the V-model and implementation activities associated with functional safety. Use them to connect a feature''s intended behavior to its hazards, requirements and verification evidence.\n\nA high score on an ML test set is only one kind of evidence. It does not establish that interfaces, fault handling, operating assumptions or the complete feature are adequate. Likewise, a short course overview is not a complete interpretation of ISO 26262 or a certification assessment.\n\nBefore the capstone, identify the boundaries of your simulated system, record assumptions and write requirements that a test can evaluate. The next module provides the requirements exercise and a model-based workflow in which to practise those skills.","capsule_id":"07c8d901-3022-4cb5-8cf8-f6d5b111fdfb","before_title":"What this module covers"},{"id":"52932f29-a468-49e4-a146-04de18886745","before_content_value":"## Start here\n\nEverything the course has covered, run as an industry workflow: **Requirements → Architecture → Control → MIL/SIL validation**.\n\n- **Requirements writing assignment**, with a worked solution — where real feature development actually begins\n- **Capstone Project 11 — AEB, ACC and LKA in Simulink**, co-simulated against IPG CarMaker\n- **MIL/SIL testing and code generation** — building the test harness, then generating embedded code with Embedded Coder\n\n**Why it matters:** this is Model-Based Design as the industry practises it. The notebooks got you the algorithms; this gets you the process — and the process is what you will be hired to follow.\n\n**Congratulations.** You have taken a feature from requirement to generated code.","content_value":"## Capstone: requirements, implementation and test evidence\n\nUse the requirements-writing assignment before reading its worked solution. Then study the AEB, ACC and LKA project resources, the IPG CarMaker co-simulation example and the material on MIL/SIL testing and code generation.\n\nKeep a trace from each requirement to its model or code component, test inputs, expected output and recorded result. State the tool versions, licenses and plant assumptions needed to reproduce the work.\n\nModel-level testing evaluates the selected model; SIL additionally executes generated code on the development computer and supports numerical comparison. Neither activity alone establishes correctness on target hardware or safety in the full operating environment. The [MathWorks SIL/PIL overview](https://www.mathworks.com/help/ecoder/ug/about-sil-and-pil-simulations.html) explains the execution distinction.\n\nComplete the accompanying capstone evidence pack and report both passing tests and known limitations.","capsule_id":"717d901c-6dcc-45c0-9514-6e66ac80f4ef","before_title":"What this module covers"}],"question_updates":[{"id":"2b3a41bc-f53a-4635-9ecd-57e17693adbe","before_question_text":"Why do most production ADAS features use classical ML rather than deep networks?","before_options":{"a":"They are more accurate","b":"They are cheaper to run, easier to certify, and often enough","c":"Deep learning does not work on cars","d":"They need no data"},"before_correct_answer":"b","before_explanation":"Latency budgets are hard and the safety case must be arguable. \"It is simpler and it passes\" beats \"it scored higher on the benchmark\".","question_text":"Which result could justify choosing a classical model over a deep network for a particular ADAS subtask?","options":{"a":"Classical models are always more accurate.","b":"The model meets measured task and timing requirements with a simpler validated implementation.","c":"Deep networks cannot process driving data.","d":"The model requires no training data or evaluation."},"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"Select a model using evidence for the specific task and implementation constraints. Neither classical nor deep models are universally superior, and model choice alone does not certify a feature. Compare measured latency and predictive performance; see https://scikit-learn.org/stable/computing/computational_performance.html.","quiz_id":"45cfb241-bef5-40b1-9949-efc9aed70854","question_type":"mcq"}],"quizzes":[{"capsule_id":"717d901c-6dcc-45c0-9514-6e66ac80f4ef","title":"Engineering check: requirements and capstone evidence","questions":[{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"In the toy stationary-target model, v=10 m/s, response delay=0.3 s and constant deceleration magnitude=5 m/s². What stopping distance excludes the clearance margin?","options":{"a":"10 m","b":"13 m","c":"15 m","d":"20 m"},"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"Delay travel is 10×0.3=3 m and braking travel is 10²/(2×5)=10 m, giving 13 m. The exercise adds a separate 2 m clearance margin.","points":1,"id":"2cb7faa8-1326-570d-a111-cfb3db3474a9","order_index":0},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A generated controller matches the model in host SIL tests. Which conclusion is supported?","options":{"a":"All sensor faults are handled safely on the target vehicle.","b":"The hardware timing requirement is proven for every processor.","c":"The full feature is certified for road use.","d":"The tested generated-code outputs match the model within the stated tolerances."},"correct_answer":"d","explanation":"SIL executes generated code on the development host and supports numerical equivalence testing. Shared model errors and target hardware effects need separate checks.","points":1,"id":"c156f1d2-f6f8-507b-aa26-0341a901315a","order_index":1},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A range signal is stale while ACC is requesting acceleration. Which design action is most defensible?","options":{"a":"Apply the specified invalid-data response and arbitration policy, then verify it with a fault-injection test.","b":"Reuse the last range indefinitely without recording its age.","c":"Assume model accuracy makes the timestamp unnecessary.","d":"Remove actuator limits so the controller can compensate."},"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"A valid-data policy, timestamps and explicit arbitration make the expected fault response observable. The appropriate response must be specified for the system, not improvised by combining feature outputs.","points":1,"id":"9a55c248-dd1b-5baa-aca2-4cb0644c9b36","order_index":2},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"Which requirement is easiest to verify in a declared simulation model?","options":{"a":"The car shall always behave like an expert driver.","b":"Braking shall feel safe in every situation.","c":"For a stated initial speed, gap, delay and plant model, the test shall measure at least the specified final clearance.","d":"The feature shall be production quality."},"correct_answer":"c","explanation":"A bounded requirement names its conditions and measurable outcome. Vague safety or quality claims do not supply a test oracle or an operating envelope.","points":1,"id":"86f704ad-0c97-5417-af77-ee267a7559c3","order_index":3}],"id":"45cc2f23-5b79-547e-a253-e450397178e4"}]},{"course_id":"dd0ce657-1483-4984-9f80-fbb81e41622d","course_title":"Advanced Vehicle Dynamics & Control: From Modern C++ to Predictive Autonomy","additions":[{"target_capsule_id":"e6f6a810-e164-4e5e-934e-258acb209ac0","title":"Study guide: compare vehicle controllers under stated assumptions","content_type":"text","content_value":"# Study guide: compare vehicle controllers under stated assumptions\n\n## Goal and preparation\n\nUse the course''s ACC, planning, estimation, LQR and MPC projects to produce a controlled comparison. Your question is not “which controller is best?” but “which design meets a stated requirement on this model, with these limits and uncertainties?” Keep the experiments in simulation.\n\nYou need algebra, basic derivatives, matrix multiplication and Python functions. Review the programming resources before modifying a control loop; use the C++ practice material when implementing the ACC project. The Simulink and MATLAB artifacts require the relevant software and licenses. The experiment below runs with Python 3 alone, so every learner can investigate one important failure mode without those tools.\n\n## Study the models before choosing gains\n\nBegin with **Longitudinal Vehicle Dynamics & Linear Controls**. State the input, output, sampling interval and equilibrium. An acceleration-command model differs from a throttle-command model; confusing them changes both units and controller gain interpretation. Reproduce an open-loop response before closing the loop.\n\nFor **Motion Planning & Control**, check whether the planned trajectory is dynamically admissible. In a low-slip kinematic bicycle approximation, curvature is `tan(delta)/L` and lateral acceleration is approximately `v²*tan(delta)/L`, with steering angle `delta` in radians and wheelbase `L` in metres. At fixed steering angle and wheelbase, doubling speed quadruples this predicted lateral acceleration. This approximation does not capture tire saturation or prove that a high-speed maneuver is feasible.\n\nIn the **Lateral Dynamics, Estimation and Optimal Control** module, separate measured signals from estimated states. State what the estimator assumes about process and measurement uncertainty, and inspect the effect of a delayed or biased measurement on the closed loop rather than only plotting a smooth estimate.\n\n## Understand what the optimization actually promises\n\nLQR selects feedback to minimize a specified quadratic cost under a stated linear model; the designer still chooses the state representation and cost weights. The [MathWorks LQR reference](https://www.mathworks.com/help/control/ref/lti.lqr.html) describes the gain and Riccati solution. LQR can account for future cost, and finite-horizon variants exist; it is misleading to describe it as having no view of the future. See the [MIT optimal-control notes](https://underactuated.mit.edu/lqr.html).\n\nStandard unconstrained LQR does not directly enforce hard actuator limits. MPC can include limits in a repeated optimization, but the constraints actually encoded, the plant approximation and solver status determine what is enforced. [MathWorks documents hard and soft MPC constraints](https://www.mathworks.com/help/mpc/ug/specifying-constraints.html). A soft constraint permits penalized violation; an infeasible problem or missed computation deadline requires a defined response. Algorithm names do not provide a universal ranking of safety, speed or effectiveness.\n\n## Runnable exercise: an actuator limit changes the problem\n\nThis toy longitudinal plant has `dv/dt = u - 0.08*v`. Input `u` is a commanded acceleration contribution, clipped to [-3, 2] m/s². A PI controller first requests 15 m/s and then 4 m/s. Compare unrestricted integration with conditional integration. The coefficients are classroom choices, not vehicle calibration values.\n\n```python\ndef run(antiwindup):\n    dt, speed, integral = 0.02, 0.0, 0.0\n    history = []\n    for step in range(1000):\n        time = step * dt\n        reference = 15.0 if time < 8.0 else 4.0\n        error = reference - speed\n        raw = 0.6 * error + 0.4 * integral\n        command = max(-3.0, min(2.0, raw))\n        can_integrate = (\n            -3.0 < raw < 2.0\n            or (raw >= 2.0 and error < 0.0)\n            or (raw <= -3.0 and error > 0.0)\n        )\n        if not antiwindup or can_integrate:\n            integral += error * dt\n        speed = max(0.0, speed + dt * (command - 0.08 * speed))\n        history.append((time, reference, speed, command, integral))\n    return history\n\nfor enabled in (False, True):\n    samples = run(enabled)\n    peak_after_change = max(row[2] for row in samples[400:])\n    assert all(-3.0 <= row[3] <= 2.0 for row in samples)\n    print(enabled, round(peak_after_change, 3), round(samples[-1][2], 3))\n```\n\nThe unrestricted integrator produces a peak of about 15.894 m/s after the reference drops, versus about 11.745 m/s with this conditional-integration implementation. The large stored integral keeps demanding acceleration after the reference change. Plot reference, speed, command and integral to explain the mechanism. [The official anti-windup example](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simulink/slref/anti-windup-control-using-a-pid-controller.html) discusses conditional integration and back-calculation.\n\n## Capstone comparison and failure analysis\n\nExtend an existing project with one nominal case, one actuator-saturation case, one sensor-delay or bias case and one model-parameter change. Keep the reference trajectory, sample interval and initial conditions consistent when comparing designs. Record tracking error, peak command, time spent saturated, computation time and the exact pass/fail limits chosen before tuning. For MPC also record solver status; for state feedback record which states were measured or estimated.\n\nUse the RL project as a further comparison only if its policy and environment can be reproduced. A larger reward does not automatically mean a smaller tracking error or fewer constraint violations. Report metrics that match the same engineering question for all designs.\n\nSubmit model equations and units, reproducible code/version information, controller parameters, scenario definitions, result plots and a failure explanation. Score out of 100: model/interface correctness (25), fair comparison and metrics (25), limit/uncertainty experiments (25), reproducibility (15), and interpretation of assumptions and limits (10). A simulation result establishes behavior in the tested model; it is not a claim about an untested vehicle or operating condition.","id":"8c47a4d9-1361-5daa-a014-95a9615e2601"}],"capsule_updates":[{"id":"89f71ecd-75a3-44b5-8b3d-f6bda261e51a","before_description":"LQR on a clean, tractable plant. It does not tune a gain — it solves for the optimal one, and the step from PID to LQR is the step from technician to engineer.","description":"Apply LQR to an altitude-control model. Define the state, dynamics and quadratic cost weights, then inspect the resulting closed-loop response and its assumptions.","learning_path_id":"0ae0fc67-4ddd-492d-9f5a-7786d7a971e6"},{"id":"8baf91a5-2203-4550-89e9-0c1645e8adfc","before_description":"A PPO agent that learns the driving policy instead of being given one. Put it beside the PID and planning work you have just done — one is derived and provable, the other is learned and often better.","description":"Train a PPO policy in MetaDrive and compare reproducible scenario results with the classical-control projects. Examine reward design and failures without assuming either method is universally better.","learning_path_id":"d4927cc0-073d-4fbb-8bf1-1fb4141c6eae"},{"id":"23845fe2-4ac2-477e-a56a-89ed6a21367c","before_description":"MPC: optimise over a horizon while respecting the steering and acceleration limits the vehicle actually has. PID reacts and LQR is optimal but blind to constraints — this is the controller that sees ahead, and it is the most expensive to run.","description":"Explore trajectory tracking with MPC. Identify the prediction model, cost, encoded constraints and solver behavior, then test tracking and constraint violations under declared conditions.","learning_path_id":"28f2fbb6-a5f3-47dc-9e94-470f47f502dc"},{"id":"561a6a5d-e458-409b-878d-1a3e3d336fe7","before_description":"The same optimal control, now steering a car in its lane. Same maths as the last lesson, real consequences.","description":"Apply LQR to a lane-keeping model. Examine state errors, speed assumptions, cost weights and steering limits when evaluating the simulated controller.","learning_path_id":"0ae0fc67-4ddd-492d-9f5a-7786d7a971e6"},{"id":"51ceb9f5-eb49-4690-86d1-79021921a7a1","before_description":"The language production ADAS ships in, plus the build and test toolchain around it. Python is where you prototype; this is where it goes to run in a car.","description":"Practise modern C++ with the associated build and test tools. Use the material to prepare for the ACC implementation and to examine ownership, testing and runtime requirements.","learning_path_id":"a41b4155-efa6-4db1-a354-bd9613953d72"}],"content_updates":[{"id":"dd881bf9-2e86-4bb5-b739-3cda732a9cd6","before_content_value":"# Welcome\n\nMost control courses teach PID, then LQR, then MPC as three unrelated topics. They are not three topics. They are one idea, arriving in stages, and each stage exists because the previous one ran out of road.\n\nThat is the argument this course makes.\n\n## What you will actually do\n\nEight projects. Adaptive Cruise Control with a tuned PID, in C++. Stability analysis of the thing you just built. An end-to-end planner feeding a controller. An RL agent that learns the policy instead of being given one. Electronic Stability Control in Simulink. LQR, twice — once on a clean plant, once steering a car in its lane. And finally MPC, tracking a trajectory while respecting the limits the vehicle actually has.\n\n## How it is arranged\n\n**Foundations** → Python, then modern C++ (production controllers are C++, and yours will be)\n**Longitudinal dynamics** → the straight-line problem, and PID\n**Planning and control** → where the trajectory comes from\n**Reinforcement learning** → the learned alternative, for contrast\n**Lateral dynamics** → the hard axis: tyres, yaw, state estimation, and LQR\n**Model Predictive Control** → control that sees ahead\n\nEach module opens with a \"What this module covers\" lesson explaining why it follows the last.\n\n## Who this is for\n\nEngineers who want to design controllers for real vehicles and be able to defend the design. You will need to be comfortable with linear algebra — LQR is not a black box, and the whole point is that you can see inside it.\n\n**Start with Module 1.**","content_value":"# Welcome\n\nThis course studies longitudinal and lateral vehicle models, feedback control, estimation and simulation projects using PID, LQR, MPC and reinforcement learning. These methods share important ideas, but have different objectives, assumptions and implementation tradeoffs.\n\nThe project sequence includes ACC, linear-control analysis, planning/control integration, PPO in MetaDrive, electronic stability control, LQR examples and MPC trajectory tracking. Reproduce the relevant examples and keep an explicit record of your model, units, sample interval, limits and evaluation conditions.\n\nBegin with the Python and C++ foundations you need, then study longitudinal dynamics before moving to planning, reinforcement learning, lateral models/estimation and MPC. You will need linear algebra for state-space models and optimal control; revisit it when the equations stop being interpretable.\n\nThe study guide on this page provides a common experiment and comparison rubric. Work in simulation and distinguish evidence obtained on a model from evidence about target hardware or a physical vehicle. There is no automatic progression in which one controller is always superior to the previous one.","capsule_id":"e6f6a810-e164-4e5e-934e-258acb209ac0","before_title":"Welcome"},{"id":"4500e16d-b812-4c15-9ac3-d6230ac71c3a","before_content_value":"## Start here\n\nEverything so far you designed. This you train.\n\nLecture and slides, then:\n\n- **Project 4 — RL car control with PPO in MetaDrive** — an agent that learns to drive from reward alone\n\n**Why it matters:** put this beside the PID and planning work you have just done. One is derived, tunable and provable; the other is learned, opaque and often better. Knowing which to reach for — and being able to defend it — is the actual skill.\n\n**Next:** the hard axis. Steering.","content_value":"## Reinforcement learning as a comparison\n\nUse the lecture, slides and MetaDrive PPO project to investigate a policy learned through interaction. Its behavior depends on the observations, action space, rewards, training distribution and environment rules.\n\nCompare the policy with a declared baseline on reproducible held-out scenarios. Report tracking or task success, failures, constraint violations and execution requirements alongside reward. Learned control is not automatically better than a classical controller, and a classical design''s guarantees apply only under its stated assumptions.\n\nThe next module introduces lateral vehicle models and estimation. Keep the distinction between a learned policy and the dynamics of the plant it acts on.","capsule_id":"3560d351-c6bc-4557-a6fb-018339a3f085","before_title":"What this module covers"},{"id":"3b2ca858-4578-4d3c-9339-d90c01878e8e","before_content_value":"## Start here\n\nLateral dynamics, state estimation, and optimal control — the deepest module in the course.\n\nLecture and slides cover the bicycle model, tyre models, ABS/TCS/ESC, and state estimation with Kalman, EKF and particle filters. Then:\n\n- **Project 5 — Electronic Stability Control in Simulink**\n- **Project 6 — Altitude control with LQR**\n- **Project 7 — Lane Keep Assist with LQR**\n\n**Why it matters:** this is where PID runs out. LQR does not tune a gain, it *solves* for the optimal one — and the step from one to the other is the step from technician to engineer.\n\n**Next:** the final generalisation — control that plans ahead.","content_value":"## Lateral dynamics, estimation and optimal control\n\nThe lecture and slides introduce bicycle and tire models, ABS/TCS/ESC concepts, state estimation and optimal control. The associated projects cover ESC in Simulink, altitude control with LQR and lane keeping with LQR.\n\nFor each example, identify the model state, input and operating assumptions. LQR computes a feedback gain for a specified quadratic cost and linear model; choosing the states and weights remains an engineering design task. Examine actuator limits and estimated-state errors before interpreting a simulated result.\n\nThe [LQR reference](https://www.mathworks.com/help/control/ref/lti.lqr.html) explains the model, weights and resulting gain. Next, study MPC as another way to pose a prediction-and-control problem, particularly when constraints need to be represented explicitly.","capsule_id":"ca8b1a70-2915-479d-8248-25f1b5d87170","before_title":"What this module covers"},{"id":"4acc9c99-2da2-4ae9-a89f-8dbb7325c4cf","before_content_value":"## Start here\n\nThe end of the line: **Model Predictive Control**.\n\n- **MPC intro** and the project explanation video\n- **Project 8 — Trajectory tracking with MPC** — clone it, run it locally\n\n**Why it matters:** PID reacts. LQR is optimal, but only for an unconstrained linear system with no view of the future. MPC optimises over a horizon *and* respects constraints — the steering limits, the acceleration limits, the road edge. It is the most capable controller here and the most expensive to run, and knowing that trade-off is the point.\n\n**You have finished.** PID → LQR → MPC, as one framework rather than three disconnected topics — exactly as promised.","content_value":"## Model Predictive Control for trajectory tracking\n\nStart with the MPC introduction and project explanation, then run the trajectory-tracking repository where the required dependencies are available.\n\nIdentify the prediction model, horizon, reference, cost weights and constraints implemented in the example. MPC repeatedly solves a finite-horizon problem and applies the next control action; the [MPC reference](https://www.mathworks.com/help/mpc/ref/mpc.html) describes this structure. Distinguish hard constraints from penalized soft constraints and check solver outcomes.\n\nLQR also optimizes future cost, with finite- and infinite-horizon formulations. Its usual unconstrained formulation does not directly impose hard input limits. MPC is useful when a suitable formulation needs constraints and prediction, but its feasibility, computation and tracking performance must be evaluated for the actual design.\n\nComplete the study-guide comparison and report both successful cases and limits.","capsule_id":"3371eeb7-1255-4125-883a-62bf3c8a2041","before_title":"What this module covers"},{"id":"225b6592-4e1a-4c64-9dbc-332e2e7ec9f2","before_content_value":"# What you have built\n\n- **Adaptive Cruise Control** with a tuned PID, as a Gradio app and in C++\n- **Stability analysis** of that controller — poles, margins, and why the tuning held\n- **An end-to-end planner and controller**, closing the loop\n- **A PPO agent** that learned the policy rather than being handed one\n- **Electronic Stability Control** in Simulink\n- **LQR**, on a clean plant and then on Lane Keep Assist\n- **MPC** tracking a trajectory under real actuator constraints\n\n## The thing you actually learned\n\nPID reacts. LQR is optimal — but only for an unconstrained linear system with no view of the future. MPC optimises over a horizon *and* respects constraints, and it costs you compute for the privilege.\n\nKnowing which one a problem deserves, and being able to say why, is the skill. It is also, almost always, the interview question.\n\n## What to do next\n\n**Push the C++.** A controller you can only demonstrate in a notebook is a controller nobody will let near a vehicle. The ACC project is in C++ for a reason — build on that.\n\n**Get your hands on a real plant.** Simulation is honest about the maths and silent about everything else: sensor noise, actuator lag, the tyre that is not the tyre in your model.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\n**Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS): Engineering & Development** is where these controllers become features. You can design an LQR lane-keeper; ADAS teaches you to write its requirements, argue its safety case under ISO 26262, validate it with MIL/SIL testing, and generate the embedded code. That is the path from control engineer to ADAS engineer.\n\n**AI Bootcamp for Autonomous Driving** is the other road — if the RL module made you want to know how the learned systems actually work, rather than treating them as the opposition.\n\nCongratulations. PID → LQR → MPC, as one framework. Exactly as promised.","content_value":"# Review your controller evidence\n\nThe projects provide opportunities to study ACC, stability analysis, planning/control integration, PPO, ESC, LQR and MPC. Mark an exercise complete when you can reproduce its behavior, explain its assumptions and identify at least one condition under which its conclusions may fail.\n\nA useful portfolio contains the plant model and units, controller parameters, test scenarios, measured outputs and a failure analysis. Compare methods on the same engineering requirement rather than assuming that a newer or more complex algorithm is better.\n\nIf you extend the C++ work, measure the relevant runtime properties and test its interfaces. If you investigate a physical plant, use an appropriately supervised laboratory setup and an explicit test plan; simulation can represent noise and lag when they are modeled, but cannot supply evidence about effects it omits.\n\nThe ADAS course adds practice with requirements and a simulated feature-development workflow. The AI Bootcamp expands the machine-learning material. Choose the next topic from a specific limitation in your current evidence, and state that limitation in your project README.","capsule_id":"9cb6c6c1-8bc3-4991-ad5c-fc8615c90988","before_title":"What next"}],"question_updates":[],"quizzes":[{"capsule_id":"3371eeb7-1255-4125-883a-62bf3c8a2041","title":"Engineering check: controller assumptions and constraints","questions":[{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"Which statement correctly distinguishes standard unconstrained LQR from a constrained MPC formulation?","options":{"a":"LQR cannot optimize future cost.","b":"MPC guarantees feasibility for every possible initial state.","c":"LQR optimizes a quadratic cost under its model, while MPC can explicitly include the chosen input and state constraints.","d":"MPC always uses less computation than LQR."},"correct_answer":"c","explanation":"LQR can consider finite or infinite future cost. Standard unconstrained LQR does not directly enforce hard bounds; an MPC formulation can include them, but its feasibility still needs to be checked.","points":1,"id":"fbe89acd-fe72-53c0-af31-5af04f45ce96","order_index":0},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A PI speed controller repeatedly requests acceleration above the actuator limit. Why can recovery be slow after the speed reference drops?","options":{"a":"Its integral can keep accumulating error while the actuator is saturated.","b":"Clipping the command automatically clears the integral.","c":"Saturation removes all plant dynamics.","d":"Integral action measures the target speed directly."},"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Integrator windup stores a large contribution even when the requested command cannot be applied. Conditional integration or back-calculation can help, depending on the actuator arrangement.","points":1,"id":"c67c797f-64e3-5f5b-a796-cea25d04e472","order_index":1},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"An MPC solver reports that the chosen hard constraints cannot be satisfied from the current state. What should the implementation do?","options":{"a":"Assume the last optimizer iterate obeys all constraints.","b":"Silently remove the bounds without recording the change.","c":"Increase the horizon until a result is returned, regardless of the control deadline.","d":"Apply the specified solver-failure policy and record the condition for investigation."},"correct_answer":"d","explanation":"An infeasible or late optimization does not provide a valid command merely because the controller is called MPC. The implementation needs a defined response and tests for that response.","points":1,"id":"10959974-2e76-5141-aacd-b13536f0499b","order_index":2},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"In a fixed-curvature kinematic approximation, lateral acceleration is a_y = v² × curvature. What happens when speed doubles?","options":{"a":"It doubles.","b":"It becomes four times as large.","c":"It is unchanged.","d":"It becomes half as large."},"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"With curvature fixed, acceleration depends on speed squared: (2v)²=4v². This approximation does not account for tire-force saturation or establish high-speed feasibility.","points":1,"id":"347eb360-8cab-536f-a326-964ac60ba2ae","order_index":3}],"id":"48b32d7d-d223-5a26-a610-35d51d8e15ea"}]},{"course_id":"699a4120-587c-48a6-bda6-570ba0b29377","course_title":"CI/CD Foundations: From Git Commit to Kubernetes Cluster","additions":[{"target_capsule_id":"78cd0331-43e9-47b4-8cb8-2f4ba5ea887e","title":"Verification lab: commit to deployed response","content_type":"text","content_value":"# Verification lab: prove which commit reached your cluster\n\nThis lab strengthens the existing Linux → Git → Jenkins → Docker → Kubernetes sequence with observable acceptance checks. Keep the linked recordings and parameterized project alongside it. The goal is an evidence chain: a source revision, a passing check, an identified image, and the response from the deployed application.\n\n## Prerequisites and study sequence\n\nUse a practice directory, Python 3, Git with a configured author identity, Docker, kubectl, and an existing local Minikube installation. Container and cluster steps need a working container runtime and downloaded images; the Python checks run independently. Record tool versions and the current kubectl context. Use a dedicated local learning cluster.\n\nFirst complete the Linux lessons and explain paths, exit status, and environment variables. Next make a Git change and inspect its diff. Study the Jenkins lessons before automating that check. Finally build the container, inspect it locally, and repeat the same acceptance check through Kubernetes. Registry publication is an extension once local delivery works.\n\nA Jenkinsfile describes stages; its agent still needs the tools and permissions those stages call. Scope registry credentials to the push stage and use Jenkins credential IDs. A pull request from an untrusted contributor must not receive deployment credentials. See the [Jenkinsfile documentation](https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/jenkinsfile/) for stage execution and credential handling.\n\n## Runnable exercise: a release with an identity\n\nCreate these files in the practice directory. The application returns its revision and has a separate health endpoint.\n\n`app.py`:\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\n\ndef route(path, revision):\n    if path == ''/health'':\n        return 200, b''ok\\n''\n    if path == ''/'':\n        return 200, f''revision={revision}\\n''.encode()\n    return 404, b''not found\\n''\n\nclass Handler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    def do_GET(self):\n        status, body = route(self.path, os.environ.get(''SOURCE_REV'', ''dev''))\n        self.send_response(status)\n        self.end_headers()\n        self.wfile.write(body)\n\nif __name__ == ''__main__'':\n    HTTPServer((''0.0.0.0'', 8080), Handler).serve_forever()\n```\n\n`test_app.py`:\n\n```python\nimport unittest\nfrom app import route\n\nclass ReleaseContract(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_release_identity(self):\n        self.assertEqual(route(''/'', ''commit123''), (200, b''revision=commit123\\n''))\n        self.assertNotEqual(route(''/'', ''commit123''), route(''/'', ''commit456''))\n    def test_health_and_unknown_route(self):\n        self.assertEqual(route(''/health'', ''commit123''), (200, b''ok\\n''))\n        self.assertEqual(route(''/missing'', ''commit123'')[0], 404)\n```\n\nRun `python3 -m unittest -v`: expect two passing tests. These check the release response, the health contract, and missing-route behavior. They complement the HTTP smoke test below; they do not establish deployment correctness by themselves.\n\nCreate a `Dockerfile`:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.12-slim\nARG SOURCE_REV=dev\nENV SOURCE_REV=$SOURCE_REV\nLABEL org.opencontainers.image.revision=$SOURCE_REV\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY app.py test_app.py ./\nRUN python -m unittest -v\nEXPOSE 8080\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]\n```\n\nCommit the files, then build and inspect the application:\n\n```bash\ngit init\ngit add app.py test_app.py Dockerfile\ngit commit -m ''Add release identity and contract checks''\nrevision=$(git rev-parse --short=12 HEAD)\ndocker build --build-arg SOURCE_REV=\"$revision\" -t \"kg-cicd:$revision\" .\ndocker run --rm --name kg-cicd-check -p 127.0.0.1:18080:8080 \"kg-cicd:$revision\"\n```\n\nLeave the container running. In a second terminal, `curl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:18080/health` must print `ok`; requesting `/` must print the twelve-character revision after `revision=`. Stop the container before continuing. The [Docker build guide](https://docs.docker.com/build/building/best-practices/) explains build context and image construction; for a release pipeline, record the resolved base-image and application-image digests as well as tags.\n\nWith the local learning cluster selected, repeat through Kubernetes:\n\n```bash\nminikube image load \"kg-cicd:$revision\"\nkubectl create deployment kg-cicd --image=\"kg-cicd:$revision\" --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -\nkubectl rollout status deployment/kg-cicd --timeout=120s\nkubectl port-forward deployment/kg-cicd 18080:8080\n```\n\nRun the same curl checks in another terminal. Capture the Pod image ID and observed revision. Loading the image is necessary because your cluster does not automatically share the host''s image store. A [Deployment](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/) manages rollout state; an HTTP response checks what the application actually serves.\n\n## Failure experiment and Jenkins integration\n\nChange the health test expectation to an incorrect value. The next Docker build must fail before producing a successful release. Restore the test, commit again, and build the new revision. Put `python3 -m unittest -v` before image publication in Jenkins, and keep the nonzero exit status intact: `|| true` would bypass this gate.\n\nIf a Pod reports ImagePullBackOff, inspect its events and image availability. If it starts but serves the wrong revision, compare tag, image ID, and build argument. If port forwarding fails, inspect Pod readiness and selected context before changing application code. Add a readiness probe in the capstone; [Kubernetes probe documentation](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/) distinguishes traffic readiness from process liveness.\n\n## Capstone and evidence rubric\n\nSubmit a repository and a one-page run record. Award 25% for meaningful checks and a demonstrated failing gate, 25% for commit-to-image traceability, 25% for the observed deployed response and readiness configuration, and 25% for recovery: a second release, controlled rollback, verified old response, and cleanup commands. A screenshot of a green job without the source revision or deployed response earns no traceability credit. Reproduce the workflow on another learner''s local environment before calling it portable.\n","id":"e0390e77-bbd1-566a-a54c-a823be68f213"}],"capsule_updates":[{"id":"0c84f8fe-a07b-42a7-8b47-1671051693e3","before_description":"","description":"Use a Jenkinsfile to make build steps reviewable with the application. Intentionally fail a check, confirm later delivery stages do not run, then restore it and compare the two build records; do not treat a green job as proof without inspecting what it tested.","learning_path_id":"becefbc1-0713-45ae-8bbf-7c537bcd9e99"},{"id":"1c886871-0758-4292-8861-ec4b4b90a7b4","before_description":null,"description":"Walk through a small change from an isolated branch to a reviewed commit and remote push. Inspect the diff before committing, capture the resulting commit ID, and explain how that ID should follow the change into its build artifact.","learning_path_id":"952cd412-a53d-4bc1-bfb8-243c6e6897e2"},{"id":"1d7b8071-23a4-48c4-a937-ce4772937397","before_description":"","description":"Describe continuous integration as an automated check of a specific revision, then map checkout, test, and build into Jenkins stages. Define which failures must stop the pipeline and preserve a log that identifies the failing stage and source commit.","learning_path_id":"becefbc1-0713-45ae-8bbf-7c537bcd9e99"},{"id":"3647292d-a48c-4c3e-8bdd-9f62264c35e2","before_description":"","description":"Build confidence with paths, files, pipes, permissions, and exit codes before running deployment commands. Use the linked Linux resource to assemble a short command log that shows where a file lives, who can read it, and whether each command succeeded.","learning_path_id":"c2bf28ca-7e77-48de-ab39-652ab9528ebe"},{"id":"3944066e-6f21-4d55-bada-c932b0618034","before_description":"","description":"Complete a small deployment and verify it from outside the Pod using a Service or port forwarding. Record the image version, readiness result, and response body, then rehearse a controlled rollback and confirm which version is serving afterward.","learning_path_id":"0a643110-a9a3-40f2-bcb6-d24f34b821cd"},{"id":"46a41fd8-8e0b-4452-a92a-621cc0f08821","before_description":"","description":"Explain how a Deployment maintains Pods and how a Service selects their endpoints. Trace labels, selectors, image identity, and ports through a minimal application, then use rollout status and Pod events to verify the result.","learning_path_id":"0a643110-a9a3-40f2-bcb6-d24f34b821cd"},{"id":"4776be97-655e-4c13-a10d-4b65339459cb","before_description":null,"description":"Set up a practice repository and complete the add, commit, status, log, and push cycle. Verify the change on the remote and leave credentials outside committed files; use the linked recording alongside your own command transcript.","learning_path_id":"952cd412-a53d-4bc1-bfb8-243c6e6897e2"},{"id":"575a15fd-b99c-49af-9f7f-e274fdfd6bfe","before_description":"","description":"Practice a repeatable command-line investigation: inspect the current directory and environment, locate a process, read its logs, and check its exit status. Keep a small troubleshooting checklist so a failed build produces evidence rather than a sequence of guesses.","learning_path_id":"c2bf28ca-7e77-48de-ab39-652ab9528ebe"},{"id":"61530bee-5ff0-479d-8376-b549177a30b0","before_description":null,"description":"Distinguish your working tree, the staging area, and committed history, then connect a local repository to a GitHub remote. Use the linked guide to explain what is shared by a push and what remains only on your computer.","learning_path_id":"952cd412-a53d-4bc1-bfb8-243c6e6897e2"},{"id":"66056e7f-9d03-4c3a-bd6c-cabb807c6ce7","before_description":"","description":"Connect a Jenkins job to the agent, workspace, parameters, and credentials it needs. Produce a short environment checklist: a pipeline definition alone does not install Python, Docker, kubectl, registry permissions, or access to a cluster.","learning_path_id":"becefbc1-0713-45ae-8bbf-7c537bcd9e99"},{"id":"72cf8170-a34f-4c7d-ba01-9e1e80a7af02","before_description":"","description":"Connect Linux processes, environment variables, and filesystem permissions to the machine that runs a delivery pipeline. Record how you would identify a running service, inspect its output, and diagnose a permission failure without relying on a graphical interface.","learning_path_id":"c2bf28ca-7e77-48de-ab39-652ab9528ebe"},{"id":"78cd0331-43e9-47b4-8cb8-2f4ba5ea887e","before_description":"","description":"Implement the parameterized project with your own repository, registry, and local cluster settings. Use the added verification lab to test the application itself, identify the deployed revision, and demonstrate that a failed check prevents delivery before presenting your final pipeline.","learning_path_id":"39a39b93-3d6f-4a71-af76-0a33b147ba36"},{"id":"793bb3e5-0bcb-4334-b2ce-b2f4ba20da3a","before_description":"","description":"Distinguish a Docker image from its running containers and Docker Hub from the local image store. Use your own registry namespace and a scoped access token, then verify that another environment can retrieve the intended image.","learning_path_id":"f9ff6723-29a9-4ede-8450-a9ab219e3a7d"},{"id":"7a5ae5d3-067b-422d-b317-3ce5c44b336f","before_description":"","description":"Turn the delivery workflow into an operational blueprint: prerequisites, stage inputs and outputs, credentials, failure gates, and recovery steps. Explain the boundary between automatically validating a change and deciding whether it should reach a particular environment.","learning_path_id":"39a39b93-3d6f-4a71-af76-0a33b147ba36"},{"id":"7e4eef38-d272-4f8d-95fe-c9b9d97b9f22","before_description":"","description":"Trace a single application change through Git, Jenkins, Docker, and Kubernetes using the linked implementation recording as a companion. Build an evidence chain from commit ID to image identity to deployed response, including the checks that can stop delivery.","learning_path_id":"39a39b93-3d6f-4a71-af76-0a33b147ba36"},{"id":"840a9885-e61a-49dc-8d5b-32239359f818","before_description":"","description":"Follow one image through build, local execution, and registry publication. Record its repository, tag, and digest, verify the exposed application response, and explain why rebuilding or reusing a mutable tag can change what another machine receives.","learning_path_id":"f9ff6723-29a9-4ede-8450-a9ab219e3a7d"},{"id":"daf58122-b6b9-4005-bc9b-c92b5535212b","before_description":"","description":"Use a local Minikube cluster to practice deployment without requiring a production account. Confirm the kubectl context and namespace, choose how the cluster obtains its image, and separate image-pull failures from application-startup failures.","learning_path_id":"0a643110-a9a3-40f2-bcb6-d24f34b821cd"},{"id":"fff16f36-63ed-4455-9b12-4f66b7c6abc3","before_description":"","description":"Read a Dockerfile as an explicit description of runtime files and startup behavior. Identify the base image, build context, working directory, copied files, and process command, then check that the container works without depending on an unmounted file from your laptop.","learning_path_id":"f9ff6723-29a9-4ede-8450-a9ab219e3a7d"}],"quizzes":[{"capsule_id":"0c84f8fe-a07b-42a7-8b47-1671051693e3","title":"CI failure gates and delivery evidence","questions":[{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A Jenkins test stage runs `python3 -m unittest -v || true`. A test fails, but the image is pushed and deployed. What is the most direct fix?","options":{"a":"Increase the number of replicas","b":"Preserve the test command’s nonzero exit status and require a successful test stage before publishing","c":"Rename the Docker image to latest","d":"Run the failing test only after deployment"},"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"The shell fallback turns a failed check into a successful exit status. A delivery gate must retain the failure so later publishing and deployment steps do not run.","points":1,"id":"23c6cca3-3095-56e5-a5bb-a5367d50bc6b","order_index":0},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"The container starts, but the application serves an older revision. Which evidence most directly identifies what is running?","options":{"a":"The latest Git commit alone","b":"The fact that the Jenkins job is green","c":"The running container’s image ID/digest together with the application’s revision response","d":"The name of the Kubernetes namespace alone"},"correct_answer":"c","explanation":"Source, build, and runtime are separate steps. Inspecting the actual image identity and served revision tests the connection between the intended artifact and the running application.","points":1,"id":"734017ba-73e6-5450-a142-8379890e7257","order_index":1}],"id":"e33a7fb4-db89-5210-af9c-4758999c65ea"},{"capsule_id":"3944066e-6f21-4d55-bada-c932b0618034","title":"Kubernetes readiness and recovery","questions":[{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A process is running but is not yet able to serve application requests. Which Kubernetes mechanism is intended to keep it out of ready Service endpoints?","options":{"a":"A readiness probe","b":"A longer image tag","c":"The Git branch name","d":"A registry access token"},"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Readiness tells Kubernetes whether a container is prepared to receive traffic. It is different from checking whether a process is alive.","points":1,"id":"38953339-c3b1-5efb-af8d-78abcbc16d73","order_index":0},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"After a controlled rollback, what is the strongest completion check for this lab?","options":{"a":"The rollback command was typed without an error","b":"The original developer says the earlier release worked","c":"The tag still has the same name","d":"Rollout status completes and a request through the deployed application returns the expected earlier revision"},"correct_answer":"d","explanation":"A rollback request is an action, not evidence of the resulting application state. Verify both workload rollout and the response that users actually receive.","points":1,"id":"b10a8b53-cc7f-5739-aad8-c02e99caae2e","order_index":1}],"id":"3bde2d37-8259-5f8b-a347-e1b3ce40874c"}],"content_updates":[{"id":"696beb6c-95aa-42a7-9a51-739f10601be7","before_content_value":"# End-to-End Project: Your Commit → Your Cluster\n\nThis guide is fully **parameterized** — set the variables below once, then every\ncommand works for ANY user, on any machine. Nothing in this project is tied to\nthe instructor''s accounts.\n\n## 0. Your variables (set once)\n\n```bash\nexport GITHUB_USER=\"<your-github-username>\"\nexport DOCKERHUB_USER=\"<your-dockerhub-username>\"\nexport APP_NAME=\"cicd-demo\"\nexport IMAGE=\"$DOCKERHUB_USER/$APP_NAME\"\nexport K8S_NAMESPACE=\"demo\"\n```\n\nPut these in your shell profile (or a `.env` you `source`) so every step below\nis copy-paste. Wherever a file cannot read your shell (Jenkinsfile, YAML), we\ninject them explicitly — that is the whole trick that makes this repeatable.\n\n## 1. The application repository\n\n```bash\ngit init \"$APP_NAME\" && cd \"$APP_NAME\"\n# app.py — a minimal web app; any language works, the pipeline is the point\ncat > app.py <<''PY''\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\nclass H(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    def do_GET(self):\n        self.send_response(200); self.end_headers()\n        self.wfile.write(b\"pipeline works!\")\nHTTPServer((\"0.0.0.0\", 8080), H).serve_forever()\nPY\ncat > test_app.py <<''PY''\ndef test_truth():\n    assert 1 + 1 == 2\nPY\ngit add -A && git commit -m \"app + test\"\ngit branch -M main\ngit remote add origin \"git@github.com:$GITHUB_USER/$APP_NAME.git\"\ngit push -u origin main\n```\n\n## 2. The Dockerfile\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.12-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY app.py .\nEXPOSE 8080\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]\n```\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t \"$IMAGE:local\" .\ndocker run -d -p 8080:8080 \"$IMAGE:local\" && curl localhost:8080\n```\n\n## 3. Jenkins credentials (one-time, per user)\n\nIn Jenkins → Manage Jenkins → Credentials, add:\n- `dockerhub-creds` — your Docker Hub username + access token\n- `github-creds` — your GitHub credentials (or use a public repo)\n\nThe Jenkinsfile refers to credentials **by these IDs**, never by value — that\nis what lets every student use their own accounts unchanged.\n\n## 4. The Jenkinsfile (parameterized)\n\n```groovy\npipeline {\n  agent any\n  environment {\n    IMAGE = \"${env.DOCKERHUB_USER}/${env.APP_NAME}\"\n  }\n  parameters {\n    string(name: ''DOCKERHUB_USER'', defaultValue: ''CHANGE-ME'')\n    string(name: ''APP_NAME'', defaultValue: ''cicd-demo'')\n  }\n  stages {\n    stage(''Test'')  { steps { sh ''python -m pytest -q'' } }\n    stage(''Build'') { steps { sh ''docker build -t $IMAGE:$BUILD_NUMBER .'' } }\n    stage(''Push'')  {\n      steps {\n        withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: ''dockerhub-creds'',\n            usernameVariable: ''DH_USER'', passwordVariable: ''DH_TOKEN'')]) {\n          sh ''echo \"$DH_TOKEN\" | docker login -u \"$DH_USER\" --password-stdin''\n          sh ''docker push $IMAGE:$BUILD_NUMBER''\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    stage(''Deploy'') {\n      steps {\n        sh ''sed \"s|__IMAGE__|$IMAGE:$BUILD_NUMBER|\" k8s/deployment.yaml | kubectl apply -f -''\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAdd a GitHub webhook: repo → Settings → Webhooks →\n`http://<your-jenkins-host>/github-webhook/` — push events.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes manifests (image is a placeholder)\n\n`k8s/deployment.yaml`:\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata: { name: cicd-demo }\nspec:\n  replicas: 2\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: cicd-demo } }\n  template:\n    metadata: { labels: { app: cicd-demo } }\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: app\n          image: __IMAGE__        # substituted by the pipeline — never hardcoded\n          ports: [ { containerPort: 8080 } ]\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata: { name: cicd-demo }\nspec:\n  selector: { app: cicd-demo }\n  ports: [ { port: 80, targetPort: 8080 } ]\n  type: NodePort\n```\n\n```bash\nminikube start\nkubectl create namespace \"$K8S_NAMESPACE\" || true\nkubectl config set-context --current --namespace=\"$K8S_NAMESPACE\"\n```\n\n## 6. Run the loop\n\n```bash\necho \"# trigger\" >> app.py\ngit commit -am \"pipeline test\" && git push\n# webhook → Jenkins: Test → Build → Push → Deploy\nkubectl get pods -w\nminikube service cicd-demo -n \"$K8S_NAMESPACE\"\n```\n\n## 7. Checklist — proof it is user-agnostic\n\n- [ ] No username appears in any committed file (only `$VARS` and `__IMAGE__`)\n- [ ] A classmate can clone your repo, set THEIR variables, and deploy\n- [ ] Rolling back is one command: `kubectl rollout undo deployment/cicd-demo`\n","content_value":"# End-to-End Project: Your Commit → Your Cluster\n\nThis project template uses your own repository and registry variables. It assumes a Linux-compatible shell, Python 3, Git with an author identity, Docker, Jenkins agents with the required tools, and access to a learning cluster. Commands and authentication still need to match your environment. Use the added verification lab for stronger release-identity and failure-gate checks.\n\n## 0. Your variables (set once)\n\n```bash\nexport GITHUB_USER=\"<your-github-username>\"\nexport DOCKERHUB_USER=\"<your-dockerhub-username>\"\nexport APP_NAME=\"cicd-demo\"\nexport IMAGE=\"$DOCKERHUB_USER/$APP_NAME\"\nexport K8S_NAMESPACE=\"demo\"\n```\n\nPut these in your shell profile (or a `.env` you `source`) so every step below\nis copy-paste. Wherever a file cannot read your shell (Jenkinsfile, YAML), we\ninject them explicitly — that is the whole trick that makes this repeatable.\n\n## 1. The application repository\n\n```bash\ngit init \"$APP_NAME\" && cd \"$APP_NAME\"\n# app.py — a minimal web app; any language works, the pipeline is the point\ncat > app.py <<''PY''\nimport os\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\nclass H(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    def do_GET(self):\n        self.send_response(200); self.end_headers()\n        self.wfile.write(b\"pipeline works!\")\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    HTTPServer((\"0.0.0.0\", int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"8080\"))), H).serve_forever()\nPY\ncat > test_app.py <<''PY''\nimport threading, unittest\nfrom http.server import ThreadingHTTPServer\nfrom urllib.request import urlopen\nfrom app import H\n\nclass ApplicationContract(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_http_response(self):\n        # Port zero selects a free local port; parallel tests cannot collide.\n        server = ThreadingHTTPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", 0), H)\n        thread = threading.Thread(target=server.serve_forever, daemon=True)\n        thread.start()\n        try:\n            with urlopen(f\"http://127.0.0.1:{server.server_port}\", timeout=2) as response:\n                self.assertEqual(response.status, 200)\n                self.assertEqual(response.read(), b\"pipeline works!\")\n        finally:\n            server.shutdown()\n            server.server_close()\n            thread.join(timeout=5)\nPY\ngit add -A && git commit -m \"app + test\"\ngit branch -M main\ngit remote add origin \"git@github.com:$GITHUB_USER/$APP_NAME.git\"\ngit push -u origin main\n```\n\n## 2. The Dockerfile\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.12-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY app.py .\nEXPOSE 8080\nCMD [\"python\", \"app.py\"]\n```\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t \"$IMAGE:local\" .\ndocker run -d -p 8080:8080 \"$IMAGE:local\" && curl localhost:8080\n```\n\n## 3. Jenkins credentials (one-time, per user)\n\nIn Jenkins → Manage Jenkins → Credentials, add:\n- `dockerhub-creds` — your Docker Hub username + access token\n- `github-creds` — your GitHub credentials (or use a public repo)\n\nThe Jenkinsfile refers to credentials **by these IDs**, never by value — that\nis what lets every student use their own accounts unchanged.\n\n## 4. The Jenkinsfile (parameterized)\n\n```groovy\npipeline {\n  agent any\n  environment {\n    IMAGE = \"${env.DOCKERHUB_USER}/${env.APP_NAME}\"\n  }\n  parameters {\n    string(name: ''DOCKERHUB_USER'', defaultValue: ''CHANGE-ME'')\n    string(name: ''APP_NAME'', defaultValue: ''cicd-demo'')\n  }\n  stages {\n    stage(''Test'')  { steps { sh ''python3 -m unittest -v'' } }\n    stage(''Build'') { steps { sh ''docker build -t $IMAGE:$BUILD_NUMBER .'' } }\n    stage(''Push'')  {\n      steps {\n        withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: ''dockerhub-creds'',\n            usernameVariable: ''DH_USER'', passwordVariable: ''DH_TOKEN'')]) {\n          sh ''echo \"$DH_TOKEN\" | docker login -u \"$DH_USER\" --password-stdin''\n          sh ''docker push $IMAGE:$BUILD_NUMBER''\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    stage(''Deploy'') {\n      steps {\n        sh ''sed \"s|__IMAGE__|$IMAGE:$BUILD_NUMBER|\" k8s/deployment.yaml | kubectl apply -f -''\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAdd a GitHub webhook: repo → Settings → Webhooks →\n`http://<your-jenkins-host>/github-webhook/` — push events.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes manifests (image is a placeholder)\n\n`k8s/deployment.yaml`:\n```yaml\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata: { name: cicd-demo }\nspec:\n  replicas: 2\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: cicd-demo } }\n  template:\n    metadata: { labels: { app: cicd-demo } }\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: app\n          image: __IMAGE__        # substituted by the pipeline — never hardcoded\n          ports: [ { containerPort: 8080 } ]\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata: { name: cicd-demo }\nspec:\n  selector: { app: cicd-demo }\n  ports: [ { port: 80, targetPort: 8080 } ]\n  type: NodePort\n```\n\n```bash\nminikube start\nkubectl create namespace \"$K8S_NAMESPACE\" || true\nkubectl config set-context --current --namespace=\"$K8S_NAMESPACE\"\n```\n\n## 6. Run the loop\n\n```bash\necho \"# trigger\" >> app.py\ngit commit -am \"pipeline test\" && git push\n# webhook → Jenkins: Test → Build → Push → Deploy\nkubectl get pods -w\nminikube service cicd-demo -n \"$K8S_NAMESPACE\"\n```\n\n## 7. Checklist — proof it is user-agnostic\n\n- [ ] No username appears in any committed file (only `$VARS` and `__IMAGE__`)\n- [ ] A classmate can clone your repo, set THEIR variables, and deploy\n- [ ] Rolling back is one command: `kubectl rollout undo deployment/cicd-demo`\n\n\n## Agent prerequisites and verification\n\nThe Jenkins agent must have Python 3, Docker access, kubectl, and an authorized kubeconfig for the intended learning cluster. Credentials configured in an interactive shell are not automatically available to Jenkins. Ensure its current context and namespace are correct before enabling the Deploy stage. Never attach production credentials to an untrusted student build.\n\nThe HTTP test above checks the actual application response instead of unrelated arithmetic. Keep its nonzero exit status as a delivery gate. After deployment, inspect rollout status and check the response through the cluster. Follow the added verification lab to carry the source revision into the image and served response.\n","capsule_id":"78cd0331-43e9-47b4-8cb8-2f4ba5ea887e","before_title":"The Parameterized Project Guide (works for every user)"}],"question_updates":[]},{"course_id":"ff819242-209a-4105-b598-afe6561ef1e9","course_title":"Advanced Motion Planning for Autonomous Driving","additions":[{"target_capsule_id":"8bec6954-2ea9-4237-8eb2-970938f497c9","title":"Planning baseline and closed-loop evaluation lab","content_type":"text","content_value":"# Planning lab: establish a baseline before adding learning\n\nUse this guide across the existing programming notebooks, global-search tutorial, FSM, lattice, PPO, and uncertainty resources. The course remains early access: the separate PPO learning path currently has no attached lessons. This exercise supplies a complete classical baseline; it does not claim that every planned learned-planning topic has already shipped.\n\n## Prerequisites and study sequence\n\nYou need Python 3 and familiarity with tuples, sets, dictionaries, loops, and functions. No additional package or GPU is needed for the exercise. Start with the Python and algorithms notebooks. Use the data-analysis and SQL material to organize results, then study the introduction lecture and presentation before modifying the planning examples.\n\nKeep three interfaces separate: a route gives a spatial objective, a behavior chooses a maneuver, and a trajectory assigns motion over time. A grid path is an intentionally simplified route example. It has no steering limits, acceleration model, or dynamic obstacles. [LaValle''s Planning Algorithms](https://lavalle.pl/planning/) develops the distinctions between discrete search, configuration space, and planning under differential constraints.\n\n## Runnable exercise: a falsifiable A* implementation\n\nSave this as `baseline.py` and run `python3 baseline.py`. Coordinates are integer `(x, y)` cells on a 6 × 6 grid. Each legal move changes one coordinate by one cell and costs one. The Manhattan heuristic is a lower bound under these movement assumptions.\n\n```python\nfrom heapq import heappop, heappush\n\nSTART, GOAL = (0, 0), (5, 0)\ndef astar(blocked):\n    if START in blocked or GOAL in blocked:\n        return None\n    heuristic = lambda p: abs(p[0]-GOAL[0]) + abs(p[1]-GOAL[1])\n    queue = [(heuristic(START), 0, START)]\n    cost, parent = {START: 0}, {}\n    while queue:\n        _, g, current = heappop(queue)\n        if g != cost[current]:\n            continue\n        if current == GOAL:\n            path = [current]\n            while current != START:\n                current = parent[current]\n                path.append(current)\n            return list(reversed(path))\n        x, y = current\n        for neighbor in [(x+1,y), (x-1,y), (x,y+1), (x,y-1)]:\n            nx, ny = neighbor\n            if not (0 <= nx < 6 and 0 <= ny < 6) or neighbor in blocked:\n                continue\n            new_cost = g + 1\n            if new_cost < cost.get(neighbor, float(''inf'')):\n                cost[neighbor], parent[neighbor] = new_cost, current\n                heappush(queue, (new_cost + heuristic(neighbor), new_cost, neighbor))\n    return None\n\nwall = {(3, y) for y in range(5)}\npath = astar(wall)\nassert path is not None and len(path)-1 == 15\nassert path[0] == START and path[-1] == GOAL\nassert not any(p in wall for p in path)\nassert all(abs(a[0]-b[0])+abs(a[1]-b[1]) == 1\n           for a, b in zip(path, path[1:]))\nassert astar(wall | {(3, 5)}) is None\nprint(''open-gap cost:'', len(path)-1)\nprint(''closed-gap result:'', astar(wall | {(3, 5)}))\n```\n\nExpect `open-gap cost: 15` and `closed-gap result: None`. The five-cell wall forces travel to the bottom gap and back up; closing its last cell disconnects the start and goal. The assertions check more than whether a path was drawn. Python''s [heapq reference](https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html) explains the minimum-priority queue used here.\n\nCompare against the course A* notebook on the same map and movement rules. If costs disagree, inspect coordinate conventions, diagonal moves, obstacle inclusion, and path reconstruction before timing the algorithms. As an extension, use a zero heuristic and compare cost and expansion counts. An admissible heuristic need not produce the same tied path, but this fixed unit-cost problem should retain the same optimal cost.\n\n## From point paths to decisions and trajectories\n\nNow inflate each obstacle by one cell in all directions and clip to the map. The narrow gap closes. Explain why a route for a point is not automatically feasible for a vehicle footprint. This grid inflation is a teaching approximation; do not label it a complete vehicle collision model.\n\nNext use the FSM notebook to write transition guards for cruise, follow, and stop. Replay a blocked lane and an oscillating distance estimate; log the state and guard at every step. Introduce a dwell condition or separate entry/exit thresholds and check whether unnecessary switching decreases without masking a genuine stop condition.\n\nFor the lattice lesson, record each candidate''s collision result, clearance, duration, maximum acceleration, and selection cost. Compare candidates only after applying your declared feasibility constraints. A small scalar cost does not compensate for a collision or an infeasible actuator demand.\n\nFor the traffic project, read the existing [Intelligent Driver Model explanation](https://traffic-simulation.de/info/info_IDM.html). Identify desired speed, time headway, spacing, and acceleration assumptions before changing a parameter. Compare identical initial conditions with two simulation time steps and inspect minimum gap, not just the animation.\n\n## Learned planning and uncertainty experiments\n\nWhen a runnable PPO implementation is available, freeze the observation and action definitions and separate training seeds from evaluation seeds. The [original PPO paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06347) describes a policy-optimization method; it is not a driving-safety guarantee. Evaluate a simple baseline and the learned policy on matching scenarios and report collisions, completion, and time taken separately from reward.\n\nInclude a lead vehicle that brakes, a blocked route, noisy state estimates, and an unfamiliar initial gap. Under uncertainty, record at least two plausible future trajectories and explain why the selected maneuver remains acceptable under the assumptions. If it does not, state the failure and the fallback rather than averaging it away.\n\n## Capstone and evaluation rubric\n\nSubmit code, environment versions, scenario definitions, seeds, and a results table. Allocate 30% to search correctness and explicit infeasibility handling, 25% to behavior and trajectory interfaces with logged constraints, 25% to matched scenario evaluation including failures, and 20% to reproducibility and honest limitations. Compare classical and learned planners only where both actually run. A reward curve alone does not earn collision-evaluation credit. Keep all experiments in simulation and describe what additional vehicle modeling and validation would be needed before any physical deployment.\n","id":"6733c243-f5c3-56d6-a195-22bcc726a57e"}],"capsule_updates":[{"id":"01312645-5c8b-4f89-b67d-b8f6ae7c1055","before_description":null,"description":"Practice the programming and data-structure foundations needed for planner implementations: lists, mappings, sets, functions, and priority-based search. Use the linked notebook to build small testable routines and explain the cost of the operations used inside a search loop.","learning_path_id":"2d558713-c0b5-486f-b1d5-a93401af46e8"},{"id":"0f3ec710-997c-4e02-94e0-3a1873ef8b20","before_description":null,"description":"Prepare to analyze planner experiments by organizing runs, computing summary statistics, and plotting outcomes. Define units and missing-value handling, keep scenario identifiers alongside results, and separate evaluation data from parameters you tune.","learning_path_id":"2d558713-c0b5-486f-b1d5-a93401af46e8"},{"id":"191393a5-fe83-4eb5-86a8-b4cfd4f85a80","before_description":null,"description":"Work through the two linked Python notebooks to review functions, iteration, indexing, and numerical data handling. Build a small deterministic example with assertions so later planning notebooks have a reliable starting point and reproducible inputs.","learning_path_id":"2d558713-c0b5-486f-b1d5-a93401af46e8"},{"id":"420f5cf8-d26a-4128-8e22-c45945b0319e","before_description":null,"description":"Use the linked FSM notebook to make behavior decisions explicit as states, transition guards, and available actions. Record why each transition occurs, test blocked-lane situations, and check whether hysteresis or a dwell condition is needed to avoid rapid switching.","learning_path_id":"6873445f-228e-4e32-905c-56f4a7739544"},{"id":"49ec9ebd-cb29-47fe-831d-dcb928555deb","before_description":"What you built, and where to take it.","description":"Audit the experiments you have actually completed and assemble a reproducible planning portfolio. Compare classical and learned methods only where both implementations and matched evaluation scenarios are available; list unfinished early-access topics as future work.","learning_path_id":"4717d6b7-9ddb-4564-84c3-3d211a14697b"},{"id":"4a0fb576-3546-4700-b2f0-04ebbbb075d7","before_description":null,"description":"Study the linked lecture with a focus on how learning changes the planner development loop. Identify what is learned, what remains part of the environment or objective, and which measurements are needed to compare a learned policy with a rule-based controller.","learning_path_id":"5848c7f1-b0e9-4323-abbf-5a093ce8ea7c"},{"id":"4b5a77b6-2ebb-45ba-aa6a-ddb1cb8ef118","before_description":"","description":"Use the two lecture resources to organize the responsibilities of motion prediction and planning. As you study, maintain a diagram of inputs, decisions, constraints, and outputs; distinguish a geometric path from a time-parameterized trajectory and the controller that follows it.","learning_path_id":"d2dbe91a-c917-41c9-b2c1-975f2fae831c"},{"id":"64f1e998-1609-4ed7-9e8f-4067d9576155","before_description":null,"description":"Use the uncertainty-planning lecture to identify which inputs are uncertain and how that uncertainty affects a decision. Formulate at least two plausible future scenarios and explain what evidence would justify continuing, slowing, or choosing another maneuver.","learning_path_id":"baead384-856f-4cba-b24e-e179c4f155a8"},{"id":"8bec6954-2ea9-4237-8eb2-970938f497c9","before_description":"What this course is, and what you will build.","description":"Orient yourself to route search, behavior decisions, local trajectories, prediction, and learned planning. This course is early access: use the resources currently attached to each lesson and distinguish an implemented experiment from a planned curriculum topic.","learning_path_id":"2d558713-c0b5-486f-b1d5-a93401af46e8"},{"id":"8f7e7ccf-aec9-421a-85ce-c8e4e606d4d6","before_description":null,"description":"Use SQL practice to make experiment records queryable: filter scenarios, group outcomes, and join configuration metadata with results. Treat this as a supporting data skill and write a query that compares completion and collision counts without counting duplicate runs twice.","learning_path_id":"2d558713-c0b5-486f-b1d5-a93401af46e8"},{"id":"ac2b000f-0ef8-494d-8e72-31ecb1206d8b","before_description":null,"description":"Implement and visualize A* on a grid using the linked global-planning notebook. State the movement model and heuristic, check obstacle avoidance and total path cost, and include an unreachable map so the planner is tested beyond a successful animation.","learning_path_id":"6873445f-228e-4e32-905c-56f4a7739544"},{"id":"b596143a-4c4a-4570-91f0-db2510ff0551","before_description":null,"description":"Work through the two notebooks and presentation while recording dependencies, random seeds, training settings, and evaluation conditions. Produce a compact experiment table rather than relying on one favorable rollout, and distinguish optimization reward from collision or completion metrics.","learning_path_id":"5848c7f1-b0e9-4323-abbf-5a093ce8ea7c"},{"id":"bf03de87-f50c-499d-a066-4fbc7be71ded","before_description":null,"description":"Use the linked algorithms notebook to connect state representation, expansion rules, stopping conditions, and complexity. Before optimizing runtime, verify that a candidate search respects obstacles, reconstructs a valid path, and reports unreachable goals explicitly.","learning_path_id":"2d558713-c0b5-486f-b1d5-a93401af46e8"},{"id":"d78954dc-c79c-49a4-9ba0-42b47afd6e7a","before_description":null,"description":"Connect a behavior choice to candidate local paths and speed profiles using the lattice-planning notebook. Compare feasibility, clearance, acceleration, and smoothness before selecting a candidate; a visually smooth curve can still collide or exceed the vehicle assumptions.","learning_path_id":"6873445f-228e-4e32-905c-56f4a7739544"},{"id":"d888ad37-8923-4552-b17f-37868ddc3ce7","before_description":null,"description":"Use the accompanying presentation to consolidate definitions and compare the planning layers introduced in this module. Write a short glossary and annotate one driving scenario with the route, behavior, trajectory, and prediction information needed at each step.","learning_path_id":"d2dbe91a-c917-41c9-b2c1-975f2fae831c"},{"id":"e37b67d1-7855-4f56-9f99-7e47c7cfed5a","before_description":null,"description":"Use the linked PPO project to identify the environment observations, actions, reward, termination rules, and training settings. Keep evaluation scenarios separate from training and compare closed-loop outcomes against a simple baseline before interpreting a high reward as good driving.","learning_path_id":"6873445f-228e-4e32-905c-56f4a7739544"},{"id":"e7f64c4c-3e48-4be8-979e-65de8494aa5b","before_description":null,"description":"Use the linked study resources to connect uncertainty representations with planner evaluation. Document the assumed prediction errors, test a scenario outside nominal conditions, and compare the chosen action and clearance rather than treating one predicted future as certain.","learning_path_id":"baead384-856f-4cba-b24e-e179c4f155a8"},{"id":"f7daf6bc-b152-4e5e-b814-056e030876aa","before_description":null,"description":"Combine the attached map, planning material, lecture resources, and Intelligent Driver Model reference into a documented experiment. State vehicle and traffic assumptions, inspect time-step sensitivity, and report what happens when initial spacing or lead-vehicle behavior changes.","learning_path_id":"6873445f-228e-4e32-905c-56f4a7739544"}],"quizzes":[{"capsule_id":"ac2b000f-0ef8-494d-8e72-31ecb1206d8b","title":"A* correctness under explicit assumptions","questions":[{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"On a four-connected grid with one-unit costs for horizontal and vertical moves, why is Manhattan distance an admissible A* heuristic?","options":{"a":"It exactly predicts every obstacle detour","b":"It never exceeds the obstacle-free minimum movement cost under these rules","c":"It includes the vehicle steering dynamics","d":"It remains admissible for any movement cost and any diagonal rule"},"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"Changing one coordinate by one costs a unit, so at least |dx|+|dy| steps are needed without obstacles. Obstacles cannot shorten that minimum. Different movement rules require reconsidering the heuristic.","points":1,"id":"4cf9f22f-a2a6-5352-a2f4-8dd38296880b","order_index":0},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"In the lab, closing the only gap in the wall produces no valid path. What should the planner report?","options":{"a":"An explicit unreachable/infeasible result under the current map and movement model","b":"A straight line through the nearest obstacle","c":"The last explored cell as a successful goal","d":"A shorter path cost to encourage progress"},"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Failure to find a feasible route is a meaningful output. Fabricating a goal-reaching path would hide a broken safety or validity assumption.","points":1,"id":"7ceaf09e-818d-510a-ad72-4f939a561d02","order_index":1}],"id":"cdefe8fc-7a49-5c5e-a51a-dc5afb4a07ec"},{"capsule_id":"d78954dc-c79c-49a4-9ba0-42b47afd6e7a","title":"Trajectory feasibility and planner evaluation","questions":[{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A route avoids occupied grid cells, but the vehicle body overlaps an obstacle while following it. What assumption was missing?","options":{"a":"That the path should have more plotted points","b":"That the reward should be larger","c":"Collision checking for the vehicle footprint and relevant motion constraints","d":"That A* should always be replaced by PPO"},"correct_answer":"c","explanation":"A point path does not account for the occupied area of a vehicle. The collision model and motion constraints must match the intended vehicle approximation.","points":1,"id":"d3b1813e-76fe-57e8-a0c8-06e87fd875f0","order_index":0},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A PPO planner has higher average training reward than a rule-based baseline. Which next result best supports a driving-performance comparison?","options":{"a":"Only the single most visually impressive rollout","b":"The number of policy parameters","c":"Reward measured again on the same training scenarios","d":"Matched held-out scenarios with separate collision, completion, and travel-time results for both planners"},"correct_answer":"d","explanation":"Reward can be improved in ways that do not improve safety or task completion. Comparable evaluation conditions and distinct outcome measures expose those trade-offs.","points":1,"id":"411c6c14-a93e-5fb8-a94f-2ca27eb559c0","order_index":1}],"id":"950508c1-854b-57c3-a422-581416a79256"}],"content_updates":[{"id":"0d257147-0235-4df0-adcf-e4c7f3108ba1","before_content_value":"# Welcome\n\nPlanning is the layer that decides what the car actually does. Perception tells you what is there; control tells you how to move. Planning is everything in between, and it is where most of the genuinely hard decisions live.\n\n## What you will build\n\nA planning stack, in layers:\n\n- **Global planning** — route-level search across the road network\n- **Behaviour planning with an FSM** — follow, overtake, yield\n- **Local planning with a lattice planner** — turning a decision into a drivable trajectory\n- **End-to-end planning with RL (PPO)** — a learned planner with no hand-written rules\n- **Closed-loop ML planners** — learned planners judged in the loop, not on a log\n- **Motion prediction** — what everything else is about to do\n- **MPDM** — deciding by simulating candidate policies forward\n\n## Why the order matters\n\nRoute, behaviour, trajectory. That decomposition is how essentially every production autonomy stack is organised, and you build each layer before seeing them compose. Then you replace the whole thing with a learned planner and argue about which was better.\n\n**Start with Traditional Motion Planning.**","content_value":"# Welcome\n\nPlanning connects a representation of the scene to the motion a vehicle should attempt. This course is early access: use the recordings, notebooks, and reading links currently attached to each lesson, and treat the broader curriculum as the direction of study rather than proof of completed modules.\n\n## A practical sequence\n\nStart with the programming foundations, then the introduction to prediction and planning. In Rule Based Motion Planning, work through grid-based global search, behavior decisions with an FSM, candidate local trajectories, and the linked project resources. Use the deep-learning and uncertainty material to examine what changes when policies are learned or future behavior is uncertain.\n\nA route, a behavior decision, and a trajectory answer different questions. Write down the inputs, outputs, assumptions, and failure conditions for each component before connecting them. The added planning lab gives you a runnable A* baseline with an unreachable-case check and an evaluation rubric.\n\n## Evaluate what actually runs\n\nThe curriculum includes PPO and other learned-planning topics. A notebook link does not establish that a trained policy, evaluation environment, or every planned module is complete. Compare classical and learned methods only when both implementations run under declared, matched conditions. Record missing dependencies or unfinished topics as future work.\n\nStart with the Python and algorithms notebooks if you need the foundations, or the introduction lecture if those are already comfortable.\n","capsule_id":"8bec6954-2ea9-4237-8eb2-970938f497c9","before_title":"Welcome"},{"id":"1640a8a5-181a-4eeb-9d7b-65a0e45b29f5","before_content_value":"# Where to go from here\n\nYou have built the planning stack twice — once classically, once learned — and you should now be able to argue for either without hand-waving.\n\n## What to do with it\n\n**Publish the lattice planner and the PPO agent side by side.** The comparison is the interesting artifact, not either one alone.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\n**Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS): Engineering & Development** puts planning where it belongs — one component of a safety-critical system that also has to perceive, control, and survive a functional safety review.\n\n**Modern Vehicle Control (PID → LQR → MPC)** is the layer immediately below you. A planner that ignores the vehicle''s dynamics produces trajectories no controller can follow, and that course is where you learn why.","content_value":"# Where to go from here\n\nAssemble the planning experiments you have actually completed. Include the map, movement model, collision assumptions, scenario settings, code, and measured results. A successful animation alone is not a complete evaluation.\n\n## Your portfolio\n\nShow a working classical baseline and an explicit failure case. Connect a behavior decision to a feasible trajectory where you have implemented that interface. If you also have a runnable PPO agent, compare it against the baseline on the same held-out scenarios and report collisions, completion, and travel time separately from reward. If that implementation is not complete, identify it as an extension rather than claiming a finished comparison.\n\nThis course is early access. Keep a list of the released resources you used and the planned topics you have not yet implemented.\n\n## Possible next steps\n\n**Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS): Engineering & Development** provides broader system context for perception, planning, control, and validation.\n\n**Modern Vehicle Control (PID → LQR → MPC)** develops the control layer. Study vehicle dynamics and tracking limits before assuming that a geometric path can be followed by a physical vehicle.\n\nUse the added planning lab''s rubric to decide which evidence is still missing from your report.\n","capsule_id":"49ec9ebd-cb29-47fe-831d-dcb928555deb","before_title":"What next"}],"question_updates":[]},{"course_id":"190adb5e-be9a-4bd5-9d34-19dd6bc23e9b","course_title":"KnowGraph''s Perception lab for computer Vision","additions":[{"target_capsule_id":"308a971a-1f4e-44b2-a66e-f3acc631425a","title":"Measured perception: geometry and failure analysis lab","content_type":"text","content_value":"# Perception lab: measure geometry before trusting a scene model\n\nUse this guide with the existing playground link, lecture, and slides. It supplies a small experiment that runs independently of playground access and gives you numbers against which to check your intuition. The purpose is to connect pixels, geometric assumptions, and evaluation evidence before moving to richer semantic, motion, reconstruction, and generative examples.\n\n## Prerequisites and study sequence\n\nYou need Python 3, arithmetic with ratios, and the ability to read a short function. No downloaded model, GPU, camera, or private dataset is required for the synthetic exercise. Use your own local notebook or a blank Colab notebook and retain its code and outputs.\n\nStart at the module overview and identify a question you can measure. Study the lecture and slides alongside the playground rather than treating an interactive result as its own explanation. Work through camera projection first, stereo geometry second, and error sensitivity third. Then choose one semantic or motion experiment and document its input, output, units, and reference data.\n\nWrite coordinate conventions at the top of the notebook: image coordinates `(u, v)` in pixels; camera coordinates `(X, Y, Z)` in meters; positive `Z` in front of the camera. The [OpenCV calibration documentation](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/d0c/group__calib3d.html) defines the pinhole relationship and separates intrinsics, pose, and lens distortion. The exercise below assumes an ideal distortion-free pair with identical intrinsics and parallel optical axes.\n\n## Runnable exercise: projection, disparity, and depth\n\nSave as `camera_lab.py` and run `python3 camera_lab.py`. Focal length is 800 pixels, horizontal principal point is 320 pixels, vertical principal point is 240 pixels, and stereo baseline is 0.20 meters.\n\n```python\nfrom math import isclose\n\nF, CX, CY, BASELINE = 800.0, 320.0, 240.0, 0.20\n\ndef project(x, y, z):\n    if z <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(''Point must be in front of the camera'')\n    return F*x/z + CX, F*y/z + CY\n\ndef depth(disparity):\n    if disparity <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(''Use a valid positive disparity in this setup'')\n    return F*BASELINE/disparity\n\nleft = project(1.0, 0.0, 4.0)\nright = project(1.0-BASELINE, 0.0, 4.0)\ndisparity = left[0]-right[0]\nassert left == (520.0, 240.0)\nassert right == (480.0, 240.0)\nassert isclose(depth(disparity), 4.0)\nassert isclose(depth(20.0), 8.0)\nfor invalid in (0.0, -1.0):\n    try:\n        depth(invalid)\n        raise AssertionError(''Invalid disparity was accepted'')\n    except ValueError:\n        pass\nprint(''left:'', left, ''right:'', right)\nfor d in (40.0, 20.0, 10.0):\n    print(f''d={d:.0f}px, Z={depth(d):.3f}m, +1px error={depth(d+1)-depth(d):.3f}m'')\n```\n\nThe first pair must project to `(520, 240)` and `(480, 240)`, giving a disparity of 40 pixels and depth of 4 meters. The final three lines should show depths of 4, 8, and 16 meters; a positive one-pixel disparity error changes them by approximately −0.098, −0.381, and −1.455 meters respectively. The same pixel error becomes more consequential at smaller disparities. These are results of this constructed geometry, not measured camera accuracy.\n\nRepeat with half the focal length and predict the projected point before running it. Then halve image resolution: scale focal length and principal point consistently. Keep baseline in meters. Deliberately use 20 as the baseline while still labeling it meters and observe the hundredfold depth error. This is a unit test for your reasoning, not a way to estimate an unknown physical baseline.\n\n## Failure cases to record\n\nThe simple depth formula assumes rectified correspondence with this disparity convention. Textureless regions, occlusions, repeated patterns, bad matching, and unsynchronized motion can invalidate the correspondence. Zero disparity should remain invalid or unbounded in your report, never silently become a nearby obstacle. A negative disparity needs inspection of conventions and validity before interpretation.\n\nProjection also changes when intrinsics are wrong, lens distortion is ignored, or a world-frame point is used as if it were already in the camera frame. Measure error at the image edges as well as the center when extending the exercise to calibration. Keep some calibration images aside for evaluation; fitting a pattern is different from showing performance on an unseen view.\n\n## Add a semantic or motion check\n\nFor a semantic extension, compare two axis-aligned boxes: reference `(10,10,30,30)` and prediction `(20,10,40,30)`, using continuous corner coordinates. Intersection area is 200, union is 600, and IoU is one third. Explain how the outcome changes when a small object shifts by the same number of pixels. A single box overlap does not establish detector precision, recall, or dataset performance.\n\nFor motion, track a synthetic point from `(100,100)` to `(110,100)` over 0.1 seconds. Its image displacement is 10 pixels and image velocity is 100 pixels/second. This does not establish meters/second without additional geometry. The [OpenCV tracking reference](https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/dc/d6b/group__video__track.html) provides optical-flow functions for a later real-image extension.\n\nWhen exploring reconstruction or generation, distinguish appearance from geometric evidence. The [NeRF paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.08934) addresses novel-view synthesis from posed images; a convincing generated view alone does not validate obstacle distance or free space. Describe exactly what evidence your chosen task supplies to a downstream planner.\n\n## Capstone and evaluation rubric\n\nSubmit one notebook containing the executable geometry exercise, a parameter sweep, one deliberately broken assumption, and a semantic or motion extension. Award 30% for correct frames, units, and numeric checks; 25% for controlled experiments and an interpretable result table; 25% for failure analysis with a justified validity mask or rejected result; and 20% for reproducibility and clear limitations. Include screenshots only as supporting evidence. Your conclusion should identify which perception quantity you measured, its reference, and what remains unknown before using it in a driving system.\n","id":"b9bd9303-03fe-575c-afb7-24085211c018"}],"capsule_updates":[{"id":"308a971a-1f4e-44b2-a66e-f3acc631425a","before_description":"Start here — what this module covers and why it follows the last one.","description":"Identify what you will measure before opening the playground: pixel projection, depth, semantic overlap, or motion. Use the added lab guide to choose controlled experiments and a results table so each interaction answers a specific question.","learning_path_id":"dc5977cf-e751-4b86-97ae-8f092701a827"},{"id":"93dc53ef-90ec-473d-b91e-83f3aea135ea","before_description":"The full lecture for The Perception Playground. The projects in this module assume it, so take it first.","description":"Use the linked lecture as the theory companion to your playground experiments. Keep track of coordinate frames, assumptions, and output units, then explain how a calibration or timing error could affect the world representation delivered to a planner.","learning_path_id":"dc5977cf-e751-4b86-97ae-8f092701a827"},{"id":"b2c3422a-0ed5-4500-8192-43451ec79d83","before_description":"What this course is, who it is for, and what you will have built by the end.","description":"Plan a practical route through the perception playground, lecture, and slides. Start with camera geometry and measurable outputs, then connect semantics, depth, motion, reconstruction, and generative topics while keeping a notebook of assumptions and observed failure cases.","learning_path_id":"2ae9d578-0990-4c6c-a633-fb1859001fdd"},{"id":"e1eb67de-513d-4379-b356-04640879731b","before_description":"What you built, what it means, and where to go next.","description":"Turn your playground observations into a small perception report with reproducible inputs, expected results, and limitations. Choose a next step in real-data perception or ADAS only after identifying which uncertainty or failure case your current experiment leaves unresolved.","learning_path_id":"4bd732a2-d361-4385-8b5d-7adf84560043"},{"id":"eb72112b-7f4f-4087-b075-cfbdc2e1db0a","before_description":"An interactive lab for the perception stack. Perception is far easier to build intuition for by changing something and watching what breaks than by reading about it.","description":"Use the linked interactive playground to vary one perception parameter at a time and record the effect. Compare your observations with the synthetic camera and stereo exercise, and distinguish an appealing visualization from a measurement checked against known geometry.","learning_path_id":"dc5977cf-e751-4b86-97ae-8f092701a827"},{"id":"fc5dbfc2-80da-4871-9546-1cdf07f51ac6","before_description":"The slides from the The Perception Playground lecture. Use them to revisit a specific derivation without re-watching an hour of video.","description":"Use the presentation to revisit the definitions and geometric relationships behind your experiments. Re-derive one projection or depth example with explicit units and write down the conditions under which that relationship stops being a useful model.","learning_path_id":"dc5977cf-e751-4b86-97ae-8f092701a827"}],"quizzes":[{"capsule_id":"fc5dbfc2-80da-4871-9546-1cdf07f51ac6","title":"Geometry, units, and perception evidence","questions":[{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"For the lab’s ideal rectified stereo pair, focal length is 800 pixels, baseline is 0.20 meters, and disparity is 20 pixels. What depth follows from Z = fB/d?","options":{"a":"0.005 meters","b":"4 meters","c":"8 meters","d":"80 meters"},"correct_answer":"c","explanation":"The units of pixels cancel: (800 pixels × 0.20 meters) / 20 pixels = 8 meters. The formula depends on valid rectified correspondence and the stated disparity convention.","points":1,"id":"e119a8e2-bcbe-59b2-ad0f-7953f2ef6634","order_index":0},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"An image is resized to half its original width and height with no crop. How should its pinhole intrinsics be adjusted for the resized pixel coordinates?","options":{"a":"Halve focal lengths and principal-point coordinates; keep the physical stereo baseline unchanged","b":"Double the physical baseline","c":"Leave focal lengths and principal point unchanged","d":"Halve the scene’s physical depth"},"correct_answer":"a","explanation":"Focal lengths and principal-point coordinates are expressed in pixels, so uniform image scaling changes them. Resizing the image does not move the physical cameras.","points":1,"id":"e3bcd928-9261-51b5-a403-73d61de323e8","order_index":1},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A tracked point moves 10 pixels horizontally in 0.1 seconds. What can be concluded directly?","options":{"a":"The object moved at 100 meters per second","b":"Its image velocity is 100 pixels per second; metric velocity needs additional geometry and assumptions","c":"Its depth is 10 meters","d":"The camera must have remained stationary"},"correct_answer":"b","explanation":"Pixel displacement divided by time gives image velocity. It does not by itself separate object motion from camera motion or establish metric scale.","points":1,"id":"2b084ad3-5f17-518b-a393-2e2ed4fe83f4","order_index":2},{"question_type":"mcq","question_text":"A novel generated view looks realistic. What evidence is still needed before treating its apparent free space as a geometric measurement?","options":{"a":"A more attractive color palette","b":"A longer text prompt","c":"A higher-resolution display alone","d":"A relevant geometric reference and an evaluation of distance or occupancy error for the intended task"},"correct_answer":"d","explanation":"Visual plausibility is not a measurement of metric depth or occupancy. Validate the quantity the downstream planner actually needs against an appropriate reference.","points":1,"id":"3af8b56d-2664-5537-a23c-23694e9c7eb2","order_index":3}],"id":"a15741df-4bfa-5bde-a5d0-06606539420e"}],"content_updates":[],"question_updates":[]}]'::jsonb;
  v_course jsonb; v_patch jsonb; v_quiz jsonb; v_question jsonb;
  v_course_id uuid; v_owner uuid; v_parent uuid; v_current text;
  v_type text; v_title text; v_published boolean; v_current_json jsonb;
  v_before jsonb; v_after jsonb; v_order integer;
BEGIN
  FOR v_course IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(v_plan) LOOP
    v_course_id := (v_course->>'course_id')::uuid;
    SELECT is_published INTO v_published FROM public.courses WHERE id=v_course_id FOR UPDATE;
    IF NOT FOUND THEN CONTINUE; END IF;
    IF v_published IS NOT TRUE THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Editorial course is no longer published: %',v_course_id; END IF;
    FOR v_patch IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(COALESCE(v_course->'capsule_updates','[]')) LOOP
      SELECT lp.course_id,c.learning_path_id,c.description INTO v_owner,v_parent,v_current
        FROM public.capsules c JOIN public.learning_paths lp ON lp.id=c.learning_path_id
        WHERE c.id=(v_patch->>'id')::uuid FOR UPDATE OF c,lp;
      IF NOT FOUND OR v_owner IS DISTINCT FROM v_course_id OR v_parent IS DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'learning_path_id')::uuid THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Editorial lesson is missing or moved: %',v_patch->>'id';
      END IF;
      IF v_current IS NOT DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'before_description') THEN
        UPDATE public.capsules SET description=v_patch->>'description' WHERE id=(v_patch->>'id')::uuid;
      ELSIF v_current IS DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'description') THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Lesson edited since audit: %',v_patch->>'id'; END IF;
    END LOOP;
    FOR v_patch IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(COALESCE(v_course->'content_updates','[]')) LOOP
      SELECT lp.course_id,cc.capsule_id,cc.content_type,cc.title,cc.content_value INTO v_owner,v_parent,v_type,v_title,v_current
        FROM public.capsule_content cc JOIN public.capsules c ON c.id=cc.capsule_id
        JOIN public.learning_paths lp ON lp.id=c.learning_path_id
        WHERE cc.id=(v_patch->>'id')::uuid FOR UPDATE OF cc,c,lp;
      IF NOT FOUND OR v_owner IS DISTINCT FROM v_course_id OR v_parent IS DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'capsule_id')::uuid
        OR v_type IS DISTINCT FROM 'text' OR v_title IS DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'before_title') THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Editorial text attribution or type changed: %',v_patch->>'id';
      END IF;
      IF v_current IS NOT DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'before_content_value') THEN
        UPDATE public.capsule_content SET content_value=v_patch->>'content_value' WHERE id=(v_patch->>'id')::uuid;
      ELSIF v_current IS DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'content_value') THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Text edited since audit: %',v_patch->>'id'; END IF;
    END LOOP;
    FOR v_patch IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(COALESCE(v_course->'question_updates','[]')) LOOP
      SELECT q.course_id,qq.quiz_id,qq.question_type,jsonb_build_object('question_text',qq.question_text,'options',qq.options,
        'correct_answer',qq.correct_answer,'explanation',qq.explanation) INTO v_owner,v_parent,v_type,v_current_json
        FROM public.quiz_questions qq JOIN public.quizzes q ON q.id=qq.quiz_id
        WHERE qq.id=(v_patch->>'id')::uuid FOR UPDATE OF qq,q;
      IF NOT FOUND OR v_owner IS DISTINCT FROM v_course_id OR v_parent IS DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'quiz_id')::uuid
        OR v_type IS DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'question_type') THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Editorial question attribution or type changed: %',v_patch->>'id';
      END IF;
      v_before := jsonb_build_object('question_text',v_patch->'before_question_text','options',v_patch->'before_options',
        'correct_answer',v_patch->'before_correct_answer','explanation',v_patch->'before_explanation');
      v_after := jsonb_build_object('question_text',v_patch->'question_text','options',v_patch->'options',
        'correct_answer',v_patch->'correct_answer','explanation',v_patch->'explanation');
      IF v_current_json = v_before THEN
        UPDATE public.quiz_questions SET question_text=v_patch->>'question_text',options=v_patch->'options',
          correct_answer=v_patch->'correct_answer',explanation=v_patch->>'explanation' WHERE id=(v_patch->>'id')::uuid;
      ELSIF v_current_json IS DISTINCT FROM v_after THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Question edited since audit: %',v_patch->>'id'; END IF;
    END LOOP;
    FOR v_patch IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(v_course->'additions') LOOP
      SELECT lp.course_id INTO v_owner FROM public.capsules c JOIN public.learning_paths lp ON lp.id=c.learning_path_id
        WHERE c.id=(v_patch->>'target_capsule_id')::uuid FOR UPDATE OF c,lp;
      IF NOT FOUND OR v_owner IS DISTINCT FROM v_course_id THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Study-guide target is missing or moved: %',v_patch->>'target_capsule_id'; END IF;
      SELECT lp.course_id,cc.capsule_id,cc.content_type,cc.title,cc.content_value INTO v_owner,v_parent,v_type,v_title,v_current
        FROM public.capsule_content cc JOIN public.capsules c ON c.id=cc.capsule_id
        JOIN public.learning_paths lp ON lp.id=c.learning_path_id
        WHERE cc.id=(v_patch->>'id')::uuid FOR UPDATE OF cc,c,lp;
      IF FOUND THEN
        IF v_owner IS DISTINCT FROM v_course_id OR v_parent IS DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'target_capsule_id')::uuid
          OR v_type IS DISTINCT FROM 'text' OR v_title IS DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'title')
          OR v_current IS DISTINCT FROM (v_patch->>'content_value') THEN
          RAISE EXCEPTION 'Study guide attribution or content changed after release: %',v_patch->>'id';
        END IF;
      ELSE
        SELECT COALESCE(max(order_index)+1,0) INTO v_order FROM public.capsule_content WHERE capsule_id=(v_patch->>'target_capsule_id')::uuid;
        INSERT INTO public.capsule_content(id,capsule_id,content_type,title,content_value,order_index,metadata)
          VALUES((v_patch->>'id')::uuid,(v_patch->>'target_capsule_id')::uuid,'text',v_patch->>'title',v_patch->>'content_value',v_order,
            '{"editorial_release":"2026-10-19","kind":"study_guide"}'::jsonb);
      END IF;
    END LOOP;
    FOR v_quiz IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(v_course->'quizzes') LOOP
      SELECT lp.course_id INTO v_owner FROM public.capsules c JOIN public.learning_paths lp ON lp.id=c.learning_path_id
        WHERE c.id=(v_quiz->>'capsule_id')::uuid FOR UPDATE OF c,lp;
      IF NOT FOUND OR v_owner IS DISTINCT FROM v_course_id THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Quiz target is missing or moved: %',v_quiz->>'capsule_id'; END IF;
      SELECT jsonb_build_object('course_id',course_id,'capsule_id',capsule_id,'title',title,'quiz_type',quiz_type,
        'is_graded',is_graded,'is_published',is_published,'max_attempts',max_attempts) INTO v_current_json
        FROM public.quizzes WHERE id=(v_quiz->>'id')::uuid FOR UPDATE;
      IF FOUND THEN
        v_after := jsonb_build_object('course_id',v_course_id,'capsule_id',(v_quiz->>'capsule_id')::uuid,'title',v_quiz->>'title',
          'quiz_type','quiz','is_graded',false,'is_published',true,'max_attempts',NULL);
        IF v_current_json IS DISTINCT FROM v_after THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Practice quiz attribution or settings changed: %',v_quiz->>'id'; END IF;
      ELSE
        INSERT INTO public.quizzes(id,course_id,capsule_id,title,description,quiz_type,is_graded,passing_score,max_attempts,is_published,order_index)
          VALUES((v_quiz->>'id')::uuid,v_course_id,(v_quiz->>'capsule_id')::uuid,v_quiz->>'title',
            'Formative practice for the study guide. Read the explanation and retry; this does not replace the course assessment.',
            'quiz',false,70,NULL,true,1000);
      END IF;
      FOR v_question IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements(v_quiz->'questions') LOOP
        SELECT q.course_id,qq.quiz_id,qq.question_type,jsonb_build_object('question_text',qq.question_text,'options',qq.options,
          'correct_answer',qq.correct_answer,'explanation',qq.explanation,'points',qq.points,'order_index',qq.order_index)
          INTO v_owner,v_parent,v_type,v_current_json FROM public.quiz_questions qq JOIN public.quizzes q ON q.id=qq.quiz_id
          WHERE qq.id=(v_question->>'id')::uuid FOR UPDATE OF qq,q;
        v_after := jsonb_build_object('question_text',v_question->'question_text','options',v_question->'options',
          'correct_answer',v_question->'correct_answer','explanation',v_question->'explanation','points',1,'order_index',(v_question->>'order_index')::int);
        IF FOUND THEN
          IF v_owner IS DISTINCT FROM v_course_id OR v_parent IS DISTINCT FROM (v_quiz->>'id')::uuid
            OR v_type IS DISTINCT FROM 'mcq' OR v_current_json IS DISTINCT FROM v_after THEN
            RAISE EXCEPTION 'Practice question attribution or content changed after release: %',v_question->>'id';
          END IF;
        ELSE
          INSERT INTO public.quiz_questions(id,quiz_id,question_type,question_text,options,correct_answer,explanation,points,order_index)
            VALUES((v_question->>'id')::uuid,(v_quiz->>'id')::uuid,'mcq',v_question->>'question_text',v_question->'options',
              v_question->'correct_answer',v_question->>'explanation',1,(v_question->>'order_index')::int);
        END IF;
      END LOOP;
    END LOOP;
  END LOOP;
END;
$quality$;
